WN.lb(18,{"mt_1VmTUxBrNd":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A right-angled triangle has shorter sides {=5*k} and {=12*k}. How long is the hypotenuse?","answer":"13*k","hint":"a² + b², then square root.","explain":"{=25*k*k} + {=144*k*k} = {=169*k*k}, √ = {=13*k}.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"The hypotenuse is {=17*k} cm and one side is {=8*k} cm. How long is the other side, in cm?","answer":"15*k","hint":"c² − a².","explain":"{=289*k*k} − {=64*k*k} = {=225*k*k}, √ = {=15*k} cm.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A park is 40 m by 30 m. How much shorter is the diagonal than walking round two edges?","choices":["20 m","10 m","0 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"The diagonal is √(40² + 30²).","explain":"Diagonal = 50 m, edges = 70 m, so 20 m shorter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says the hypotenuse of a 3-4 triangle is 7. What went wrong?","choices":["He added the lengths, not the squares","He forgot to double","Nothing, 7 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Square first.","explain":"9 + 16 = 25 and √25 = 5. Adding the lengths gives 7, which is too long."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A TV screen is {=16*k} inches wide and {=12*k} inches tall. How long is its diagonal, in inches?","answer":"20*k","hint":"Square, add, square root.","explain":"{=256*k*k} + {=144*k*k} = {=400*k*k}, √ = {=20*k}.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"If the angle between sides 3 and 4 is 120°, the third side is…","choices":["longer than 5","exactly 5","shorter than 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Opening the angle pushes the ends apart.","explain":"A wider angle makes c longer, so c² is bigger than a² + b²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A ramp rises {=7*k} m over a flat distance of {=24*k} m. How long is the ramp, in m?","answer":"25*k","hint":"The ramp is the hypotenuse.","explain":"{=49*k*k} + {=576*k*k} = {=625*k*k}, √ = {=25*k} m.","params":{"k":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"The two shorter sides of a right-angled triangle are {k}×3 cm and {k}×4 cm. How long is the hypotenuse, in cm?","answer":"5*k","hint":"Square both, add, then take the square root.","explain":"({=3*k})² + ({=4*k})² = {=25*k*k}, and √{=25*k*k} = {=5*k} cm.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A ladder {=13*k} m long has its foot {=5*k} m from a wall. How high does it reach, in m?","answer":"12*k","hint":"Subtract the squares for a shorter side.","explain":"({=13*k})² − ({=5*k})² = {=144*k*k}, √{=144*k*k} = {=12*k} m.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which side is the hypotenuse?","choices":["The side opposite the right angle","The bottom side","The shortest side"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is always the longest side.","explain":"The hypotenuse is opposite the right angle, and it is the longest side."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A triangle has sides 6, 8 and 11. Is it right-angled?","choices":["No: 36 + 64 = 100, not 121","Yes: 6 + 8 > 11","Yes, every triangle is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check a² + b² against c².","explain":"36 + 64 = 100, but 11² = 121, so it is not right-angled (the angle is wider than 90°)."}],"explain":{"kid":["The longest side of a right-angled triangle is the hypotenuse. It is opposite the right angle.","Build a square on each side. The two small squares together fill the big one exactly: a² + b² = c².","To find the long side, add the squares, then take the square root. To find a short side, subtract the squares, then take the square root."],"adult":["Pythagoras' theorem holds only for right-angled triangles: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse. If the angle is wider than 90°, c² is bigger than a² + b²; if narrower, smaller.","The classic error is working with lengths instead of areas: 3 + 4 is not 5. Square, add or subtract, then square root. A quick check: the hypotenuse must be the longest side."]},"familyTip":"Measure a door or a TV: its width and height, then work out the diagonal and check it with a tape measure.","keyIdea":"In a right-angled triangle the squares on the two shorter sides add up to the square on the longest side: a² + b² = c².","visual":{"type":"squares","caption":"Square numbers are areas: 3² = 9, 4² = 16, 5² = 25. Pythagoras adds areas like these.","opts":{"n":5}},"lab":{"type":"flagpyth","caption":"Flagship lab: the park shortcut. Drag the squares on the sides into the square on the hypotenuse.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XJJlJ6IQHx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The point on the unit circle at 210° is (−√3/2, −1/2). What are sin 210° and cos 210°?","choices":["sin 210° = −1/2 and cos 210° = −√3/2","sin 210° = −√3/2 and cos 210° = −1/2","sin 210° = 1/2 and cos 210° = √3/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"The first coordinate is cos θ and the second is sin θ.","explain":"x = cos 210° = −√3/2 and y = sin 210° = −1/2. Both are negative because 210° is in quadrant III."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is cos θ negative for angles in the second quadrant?","choices":["Points there are left of the y-axis, so their x-coordinate is negative","Angles bigger than 90° always have a negative cosine","Sine and cosine swap signs every 90°"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos θ is one of the point's coordinates. Which one, and where is the point?","explain":"cos θ is the x-coordinate, and every point in quadrant II is left of the y-axis. Size alone is not the reason: cos 300° = 1/2 is positive."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"An angle of {a}° is in quadrant III. What is its reference angle, in degrees?","answer":"a-180","hint":"In quadrant III, measure past the negative x-axis, at 180°.","explain":"{a}° − 180° = {=a-180}°, so the reference angle is {=a-180}°. Sine and cosine are both negative there.","params":{"a":[181,269]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Which angle between 0° and 360° lands on the same point of the unit circle as {=a+360*k}°?","answer":"a","hint":"Take off full turns of 360° until the angle is less than 360°.","explain":"Subtract {=360*k}°, which is {k} full turns: {=a+360*k}° − {=360*k}° = {a}°.","params":{"a":[10,350],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A point on the unit circle is at (0.6, −0.8). Which statement is true?","choices":["cos θ = 0.6 and sin θ = −0.8, so θ is in quadrant IV","cos θ = −0.8 and sin θ = 0.6, so θ is in quadrant II","cos θ = 0.6 and sin θ = −0.8, so θ is in quadrant II"],"answer":0,"hint":"x is cos θ and y is sin θ. Right of the y-axis and below the x-axis is which quadrant?","explain":"x = 0.6 = cos θ and y = −0.8 = sin θ. Right and below is quadrant IV. Check: 0.6² + 0.8² = 0.36 + 0.64 = 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In a right triangle with hypotenuse 1, which length is the side adjacent to the acute angle θ?","choices":["cos θ","sin θ","tan θ"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos θ = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, and the hypotenuse is 1.","explain":"Adjacent ÷ 1 = cos θ, so the adjacent side has length cos θ. That is why the unit circle's x-coordinate is cos θ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is sin(−90°)?","choices":["−1","1","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"A negative angle turns clockwise. Where does a quarter turn clockwise from (1, 0) end?","explain":"Clockwise 90° ends at (0, −1), the same point as 270°, so sin(−90°) = −1 and cos(−90°) = 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which angle has the same sine as 40°?","choices":["140°","220°","320°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sine is the height. Which other point on the circle is at the same height as 40°?","explain":"180° − 40° = 140° is the mirror image across the y-axis, at the same height. 220° and 320° are below the x-axis, so their sines are negative."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A point on the unit circle in quadrant I has cos θ = 0.28. What is sin θ?","choices":["0.96","0.72","0.28"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every point is 1 from the centre, so cos²θ + sin²θ = 1.","explain":"sin²θ = 1 − 0.28² = 1 − 0.0784 = 0.9216, so sin θ = 0.96 (positive in quadrant I). 0.72 comes from 1 − 0.28, which forgets the squares."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"On the unit circle, what are the coordinates of the point at angle θ?","choices":["(cos θ, sin θ)","(sin θ, cos θ)","(θ, 1)"],"answer":0,"hint":"The radius is a hypotenuse of length 1. Which side is adjacent to θ: across or up?","explain":"The across distance is adjacent ÷ 1 = cos θ, and the height is opposite ÷ 1 = sin θ. So the point is (cos θ, sin θ)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An angle of {a}° is in quadrant II. What is its reference angle, in degrees?","answer":"180-a","hint":"In quadrant II, measure the gap between the radius and the negative x-axis, at 180°.","explain":"{a}° is {=180-a}° short of 180°, so the reference angle is {=180-a}°. The point is at the same height as the point at {=180-a}°.","params":{"a":[91,179]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Turning clockwise by {a}° gives the angle −{a}°. Which angle between 0° and 360° lands on the same point?","answer":"360-a","hint":"A full turn is 360°. Going back {a}° from a full turn ends in the same place.","explain":"Clockwise {a}° ends where anticlockwise 360° − {a}° = {=360-a}° does, so both angles have the same sine and cosine.","params":{"a":[1,359]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is true for an angle in quadrant III, between 180° and 270°?","choices":["cos θ < 0 and sin θ < 0","cos θ < 0 and sin θ > 0","cos θ > 0 and sin θ < 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quadrant III is bottom left. Is x positive or negative there? And y?","explain":"Bottom left means left of the y-axis (x < 0) and below the x-axis (y < 0), so both cos θ and sin θ are negative."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What is cos 150°?","choices":["−√3/2","√3/2","−1/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the reference angle, then ask whether the point is left or right of the y-axis.","explain":"The reference angle is 30° and the point is left of the y-axis, so cos 150° = −cos 30° = −√3/2 ≈ −0.866. −1/2 would be cos 120°."}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw a circle with radius 1 and its centre at (0, 0). Start a point at (1, 0) and turn it round the circle the opposite way to a clock's hands. Call the angle it turns θ.","Read the point's place. How far across it is, x, is called cos θ. How far up it is, y, is called sin θ.","On the left side of the circle, x is less than zero, so cos θ is too. Below the middle line, y is less than zero, so sin θ is too.","At 150°, the point is up and to the left. So cos 150° is negative and sin 150° is positive."],"adult":["Take the circle x² + y² = 1. Turn a radius anticlockwise from the positive x-axis through θ. The end point is defined to be (cos θ, sin θ). For an acute angle this matches SOH CAH TOA: the radius is a hypotenuse of length 1, so the adjacent side is x and the opposite side is y.","The definition works for any angle: 150°, 210°, negative angles (turned clockwise) and angles over 360°. Angles that differ by a full turn, 360° or 2π radians, land on the same point, so sine and cosine repeat.","The quadrant gives the sign. Cosine is positive in quadrants I and IV, right of the y-axis. Sine is positive in I and II, above the x-axis. The size comes from the reference angle, the acute angle to the x-axis. So cos 150° = −cos 30° = −√3/2 and sin 210° = −sin 30° = −1/2.","Every point is 1 from the centre, so cos²θ + sin²θ = 1 for all θ. Common mistakes: swapping the coordinates (cos is x, not y), using the reference angle's signs in every quadrant, turning clockwise for a positive angle, and thinking sine and cosine stop at 90°."]},"familyTip":"Hold a pencil flat on a paper plate, from the centre to the right edge. Turn it anticlockwise to about 150° and ask: is the tip left or right of centre, above or below? That gives the signs of cos 150° and sin 150° before any calculator.","keyIdea":"On the unit circle (radius 1, centre at the origin), the point reached by turning θ anticlockwise from the positive x-axis has coordinates (cos θ, sin θ). This defines sine and cosine for every angle, and the quadrant decides their signs.","visual":{"type":"geolab","caption":"Legs 8 and 6, hypotenuse 10: cos = 0.8, sin = 0.6. Scale the hypotenuse to 1 and the legs are the unit-circle point (0.8, 0.6).","opts":{"pts":[[1,1],[9,1],[9,7]],"grid":10}},"lab":{"type":"flagunit","caption":"Flagship lab: turn a point round the unit circle. Its x is cos θ, its y is sin θ, and the graph draws both as you go.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_FhJ3b20GC4":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Is a diameter a chord?","choices":["Yes: it joins two points on the circle, passing through the centre","No: chords cannot pass through the centre","No: a diameter is an arc"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the definition of a chord. Does a diameter fit it?","explain":"A chord is any straight line joining two points on the circle. A diameter does that, through the centre, so it is the longest chord."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"One straight cut across a round cake, away from the centre, cuts off a small piece. What is that piece?","choices":["A segment","A sector","A tangent"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many straight edges does the piece have, and are they radii?","explain":"The piece is bounded by one chord (the cut) and an arc, so it is a segment. A sector would need two radii."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The diameter of a circle is {d} cm. How long is its radius, in cm?","answer":"d/2","hint":"A diameter is made of two radii end to end through the centre.","explain":"The radius is half the diameter: {d} ÷ 2 = {=d/2} cm.","params":{"d":[4,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"OT is a radius and TP is part of the tangent at T. In triangle OTP, angle TOP is {a}°. What is angle OPT, in degrees?","answer":"90-a","hint":"The radius meets the tangent at 90°, so angle OTP is 90°. Then use the angle sum of a triangle.","explain":"Angle OTP is 90°. The angles add up to 180°, so angle OPT is 180 − 90 − {a} = {=90-a}°.","params":{"a":[20,70]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The minor arc AB is 140°. Which statement is true?","choices":["The major arc AB is 220°","The major arc AB is 140°","The minor arc is the longer of the two"],"answer":0,"hint":"Minor means less than half. The two arcs make a full turn of 360°.","explain":"360° − 140° = 220°, so the major arc is 220°. The minor arc is always the shorter one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the difference between a sector and a segment?","choices":["A sector has two radii as edges; a segment is cut off by a chord","A sector is cut off by a chord; a segment has two radii as edges","There is no difference"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one looks like a slice of pizza from the middle?","explain":"A sector is bounded by two radii and an arc. A segment is bounded by a chord and an arc."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A straight line cuts a circle at two points. What is the part of the line inside the circle?","choices":["A chord","A tangent","An arc"],"answer":0,"hint":"Its two ends are on the circle and it is straight.","explain":"The inside part joins two points on the circle with a straight line, so it is a chord. A tangent would meet the circle only once."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A straight line meets a circle at exactly one point and does not cross it. What is the line?","choices":["A tangent","A chord","A radius"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line only touches the circle and stays outside it?","explain":"That is a tangent. A chord meets the circle at two points, and a radius starts at the centre."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these describes a chord?","choices":["A straight line joining two points on the circle","A curved part of the circle's edge","A line from the centre to the edge"],"answer":0,"hint":"A chord is straight, and both of its ends are on the circle.","explain":"A chord joins two points on the circle with a straight line. The curved part is an arc, and a centre-to-edge line is a radius."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A circle has radius {r} cm. How long is the longest chord you can draw in it, in cm?","answer":"2*r","hint":"The longest chord passes through the centre. What is that chord called?","explain":"The longest chord is the diameter, which is two radii: 2 × {r} = {=2*r} cm.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Points A and B split a circle into two arcs. The minor arc AB is {a}°. What is the major arc AB, in degrees?","answer":"360-a","hint":"The two arcs together go all the way round the circle, which is 360°.","explain":"The two arcs add up to 360°, so the major arc is 360 − {a} = {=360-a}°.","params":{"a":[20,170]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A region of a circle is bounded by two radii and an arc. What is it called?","choices":["A sector","A segment","A chord"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a slice of pizza cut from the middle. Which name goes with two radii?","explain":"Two radii and an arc make a sector. A segment is cut off by a chord, and a chord is a line, not a region."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How many points does a tangent share with its circle?","choices":["Exactly 1","Exactly 2","None"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture a ruler resting against a coin. Where do they meet?","explain":"A tangent touches the circle at exactly one point. A line meeting it at 2 points cuts through it, and one meeting it at none misses."}],"explain":{"kid":["Think of a round pizza. The crust all the way round is the circumference. A line from the middle to the crust is a radius.","Draw a straight line from one point on the crust to another. That line is a chord. The longest chord goes through the middle. It is the diameter.","A bit of the curved crust is an arc. Two points split the crust into a short arc and a long arc. The short one is the minor arc. The long one is the major arc.","Cut a slice from the middle, with two straight cuts. That slice is a sector. Make one straight cut across the edge instead. The piece you cut off is a segment. A ruler that just rests against the edge, touching at one spot, is a tangent."],"adult":["Circles have named parts beyond radius and diameter. A chord is a straight line segment whose two ends lie on the circle. A diameter is a chord that passes through the centre, so it is the longest possible chord, twice the radius.","An arc is part of the circumference between two points. Any two points make two arcs: the minor arc, less than half the circle, and the major arc, more than half. Their angles at the centre add up to 360°, so a 100° minor arc goes with a 260° major arc.","A sector is the region between two radii and the arc joining their ends, shaped like a slice of pizza. A segment is the region between a chord and its arc, with no radii as edges. Each comes in a minor and a major version.","A tangent is a straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point and does not cross it. The radius drawn to that point meets the tangent at 90°. A line that crosses the circle at two points is not a tangent; the part of it inside the circle is a chord. Common mistakes: swapping sector and segment; thinking a diameter is not a chord; choosing the major arc by its position on the page instead of its size; and calling a line that cuts through the circle a tangent."]},"familyTip":"Cut a paper plate or a tortilla. Make one cut from the edge to the middle and back out to make a slice, and one straight cut across the edge. Ask which piece is the sector and which is the segment, and how they know.","keyIdea":"A chord is a straight line joining two points on a circle; the diameter is the longest chord, through the centre. An arc is part of the curved edge: the minor arc is less than half, the major arc more. A sector is a region bounded by two radii and an arc; a segment is a region cut off by a chord. A tangent touches the circle at exactly one point, at 90° to the radius there.","worked":{"title":"A and B are points on a circle with centre O, and the minor arc AB is 70°. Name the parts and find the major arc.","steps":["The straight line AB joins two points on the circle, so it is a chord. It misses O, so it is not a diameter.","The minor arc AB is the short way round: 70°. The major arc goes the long way: 360° − 70° = 290°.","The region between OA, OB and the minor arc is the minor sector. The region between chord AB and the minor arc is the minor segment.","A tangent at A would touch the circle only at A, at 90° to the radius OA."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each part a line, a region or part of the curved edge? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Straight line","Region (an area)","Curved edge"],"items":[["Chord",0],["Diameter",0],["Tangent",0],["Radius",0],["Sector",1],["Segment",1],["Minor arc",2],["Major arc",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagchord","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a chord until it becomes a diameter, pick the major arc, find the sector and the segment, and slide a line until it is a tangent.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HXwk3pTM1W":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which expression gives the arc length of a sector with angle θ and radius r?","choices":["θ/360 × 2πr","θ/360 × πr²","θ/100 × 2πr"],"answer":0,"hint":"An arc is part of the edge, so start from the circumference.","explain":"The arc is the fraction θ/360 of the circumference 2πr. πr² gives an area, and a full turn is 360°, not 100."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Find the area of a 72° sector of radius 10 cm, to 1 decimal place.","answer":62.8,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"72/360 is 1/5. Find 1/5 of the full circle's area, π × 10².","explain":"1/5 × π × 100 = 20π, which is about 62.8 cm²."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The radius of a sector doubles and the angle stays the same. What happens to its area?","choices":["It becomes 4 times as big","It doubles","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Area uses r². What is (2r)²?","explain":"(2r)² = 4r², so the area is 4 times as big. The arc only doubles, because it uses r, not r²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Write the arc of a {=30*k}° sector of radius {r} cm as a multiple of π. Type the number in front of π.","answer":"k*r/6","hint":"Work out {=30*k}/360 of 2π × {r}.","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × 2 × {r} = {=k*r/6}, so the arc is {=k*r/6}π cm.","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[2,12]},"where":"(k*r)%6==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ali finds the arc of a 60° sector with r = 6 cm as 60/360 × π × 6² = 18.8 cm. What went wrong?","choices":["He used the area formula; the arc is 60/360 × 2π × 6 ≈ 6.3 cm","He should divide by 100","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is an arc a length or an area?","explain":"An arc is a length, so it is a fraction of 2πr: 1/6 × 37.7 ≈ 6.3 cm. 18.8 cm² is the sector's area."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sector of radius 6 cm has an arc of 5π cm. What is its angle in degrees?","answer":150,"hint":"Divide the arc by the whole circumference, 12π, then multiply by 360.","explain":"5π ÷ 12π = 5/12, and 5/12 of 360° is 150°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the perimeter of a sector?","choices":["The arc length plus two radii","The arc length only","The arc length plus one radius"],"answer":0,"hint":"Trace round the edge of a pizza slice. How many straight edges are there?","explain":"A sector has one curved edge, the arc, and two straight edges, both radii. Add all three."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Write the area of a {=30*k}° sector of radius {r} cm as a multiple of π. Type the number in front of π.","answer":"k*r*r/12","hint":"Work out {=30*k}/360 of π × {r}².","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × {=r*r} = {=k*r*r/12}, so the area is {=k*r*r/12}π cm².","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[2,8]},"where":"(k*r*r)%12==0"}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A sector has angle {=30*k}° and radius {r} cm. Its arc length is how many π cm? (Type the number in front of π.)","answer":"k*r/6","hint":"Fraction of the circle: {=30*k}/360. Multiply it by 2r = {=2*r}.","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × 2π × {r} = {=k*r/6}π cm. The fraction of the circle times the whole edge 2πr.","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[3,12]},"where":"(k*r)%3==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A sector has angle {=30*k}° and radius {r} cm. Its area is how many π cm²? (Type the number in front of π.)","answer":"k*r*r/12","hint":"Fraction of the circle: {=30*k}/360. Multiply it by r² = {=r*r}.","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × π × {r}² = {=k*r*r/12}π cm². The fraction of the circle times the whole area πr².","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[2,10]},"where":"(k*r*r)%3==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What fraction of the whole circle is a 72° sector?","choices":["1/5","72/100","1/72"],"answer":0,"hint":"A full turn is 360°. Simplify 72/360.","explain":"72/360 = 1/5, because 5 × 72 = 360. Dividing by 100 treats the angle like a percentage."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Find the perimeter of a 90° sector of radius 10 cm, to 1 decimal place.","answer":35.7,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"Perimeter = arc + two radii. The arc is 1/4 of 2π × 10.","explain":"Arc = 1/4 × 2π × 10 = 15.7 cm. Add the two straight edges: 15.7 + 10 + 10 = 35.7 cm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A sector of radius {r} cm has an arc of {m}π cm. What is its angle in degrees?","answer":"180*m/r","hint":"Fraction = arc ÷ 2πr = {m}π ÷ {=2*r}π. Then multiply by 360.","explain":"{m}π ÷ (2π × {r}) × 360 = {m} ÷ {=2*r} × 360 = {=180*m/r}°.","params":{"m":[1,9],"r":[2,12]},"where":"(180*m)%r==0 && 180*m/r<360"}],"explain":{"kid":["A sector is a slice of a circle, like a slice of pizza. Its angle θ is at the centre. A full turn is 360°.","First find the fraction. A 90° slice is 90/360, so a quarter. A 72° slice is 72/360, so one fifth.","The arc is the curved crust. It is that fraction of the whole edge, 2πr. With r = 6 cm a quarter arc is 37.7 ÷ 4 = 9.4 cm.","The area is that fraction of the whole circle, πr². With r = 6 cm a quarter slice is 113.1 ÷ 4 = 28.3 cm²."],"adult":["Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and sector area = θ/360 × πr². Both are the same fraction of a whole-circle formula; only the whole changes, from a length to an area.","Scaling shows the difference. Doubling θ doubles both. Doubling r doubles the arc but multiplies the area by 4, because r is squared.","Working backwards uses the same fraction. An arc of 15.7 cm on r = 6 cm is 15.7 ÷ 37.7 ≈ 0.417 of the edge, so θ ≈ 0.417 × 360 = 150°. The perimeter of a sector is the arc plus two radii.","Common mistakes: dividing θ by 100 instead of 360; using πr² for an arc or 2πr for an area; using πr as the circumference; forgetting to square r; leaving out the two radii in a perimeter; and rounding the fraction early, which drifts the final answer."]},"familyTip":"Cut a paper plate into equal slices: 4, then 6, then 8. Measure the whole rim with string first. Ask how long the crust of one slice must be, then check with the string. What happens to the crust if the plate is twice as wide?","keyIdea":"A sector with angle θ is θ/360 of the circle. Its arc is that fraction of 2πr, and its area is that fraction of πr².","worked":{"title":"Find the arc length and area of a 72° sector of radius 10 cm.","steps":["Fraction of the circle: 72/360 = 1/5.","Arc length: 1/5 × 2π × 10 = 4π ≈ 12.6 cm.","Area: 1/5 × π × 10² = 20π ≈ 62.8 cm².","Check: 5 such sectors make the circle. 5 × 12.6 = 62.8 cm ≈ 2π × 10, and 5 × 62.8 = 314 cm² ≈ π × 10²."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each expression a length or an area? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A length (cm)","An area (cm²)"],"items":[["θ/360 × 2πr",0],["2πr",0],["arc + 2r",0],["θ/360 × πr²",1],["πr²",1],["¼ × π × 6²",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagarc","caption":"Flagship lab: drag the angle and radius of a sector and watch its arc and area as a lit part of the whole circle laid flat; tile copies to see the fraction.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qm2CQajR6R":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many radians make a full turn?","choices":["2π, about 6.28","360","π, about 3.14"],"answer":0,"hint":"The edge is 2πr. How many radii is that?","explain":"The circumference 2πr divided by r is 2π, so a full turn is 2π ≈ 6.28 rad. π rad is only half a turn."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Convert {k}π/4 radians to degrees.","answer":"45*k","hint":"π/4 rad = 180 ÷ 4 = 45°. Multiply by {k}.","explain":"{k}π/4 × 180/π = {k} × 45 = {=45*k}°.","params":{"k":[3,15]},"where":"k%2==1"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"An arc of {s} cm makes an angle of {=k/2} rad at the centre. Find the radius in cm.","answer":"2*s/k","hint":"Rearrange s = rθ: divide the arc by the angle in radians to get r.","explain":"r = s ÷ θ = {s} ÷ {=k/2} = {=2*s/k} cm.","params":{"s":[3,15],"k":[1,6]},"where":"(200*s)%k==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam uses s = rθ for a 60° arc with r = 5 cm and gets 5 × 60 = 300 cm. What went wrong?","choices":["θ must be in radians: 60° = π/3, so s = 5π/3 ≈ 5.24 cm","He should divide by 60","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"s = rθ only works when θ is in radians.","explain":"60° = π/3 rad, so s = 5 × π/3 ≈ 5.24 cm. Putting degrees into s = rθ gives an arc far longer than the whole circle (31.4 cm)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Convert 1 radian to degrees, to 1 decimal place.","answer":57.3,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"π rad = 180°, so 1 rad = 180 ÷ π.","explain":"1 rad = 180 ÷ π = 57.2957…, so about 57.3°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Write 240° in radians.","choices":["4/3","4π/3","2π/3"],"answer":1,"hint":"240 ÷ 180 = 4/3. Do not lose the π.","explain":"240/180 = 4/3, so 240° = 4π/3 rad. 4/3 drops the π, and 2π/3 is only 120°."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Convert 2.5 radians to degrees, to the nearest degree.","answer":143,"tolerance":0.5,"hint":"Multiply by 180/π, about 57.3.","explain":"2.5 × 180/π = 143.24…, so about 143°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Circles of radius 3 cm and 6 cm each have an arc that makes 1.2 rad at the centre. Which is true?","choices":["Both arcs are 1.2 cm long","The arcs are 3.6 cm and 7.2 cm, and the angle is the same","The bigger circle's angle is 2.4 rad"],"answer":1,"hint":"Use s = rθ for each circle. Does the angle change?","explain":"s = 3 × 1.2 = 3.6 cm and s = 6 × 1.2 = 7.2 cm. The arc doubles with the radius, but s ÷ r is 1.2 rad for both."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"How many degrees is {k}π/12 radians?","answer":"15*k","hint":"π rad = 180°, so π/12 rad = 180 ÷ 12 = 15°. Multiply by {k}.","explain":"{k}π/12 × 180/π = {k} × 15 = {=15*k}°.","params":{"k":[5,23]},"where":"k%2==1 && k%3!=0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the arc length when r = {r} cm and θ = {=k/2} rad.","answer":"r*k/2","hint":"θ is already in radians, so use s = rθ with no 360.","explain":"s = rθ = {r} × {=k/2} = {=r*k/2} cm.","params":{"r":[2,12],"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is one radian?","choices":["The angle where the arc is as long as the radius","1/360 of a full turn","The angle where the arc is 1 cm long"],"answer":0,"hint":"A radian compares the arc with the radius of the same circle.","explain":"One radian is the angle at the centre when the arc equals the radius. 1/360 of a turn is one degree, and 1 cm depends on the circle's size."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"An arc of {s} cm lies on a circle of radius {r} cm. Find θ in radians.","answer":"s/r","hint":"θ = s ÷ r: how many radius-lengths fit along the arc?","explain":"θ = s ÷ r = {s} ÷ {r} = {=s/r} rad.","params":{"s":[3,20],"r":[2,10]},"where":"(s*100)%r==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Convert 135° to radians.","choices":["3π/4","3/4","135π"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide by 180°, then multiply by π.","explain":"135 ÷ 180 = 3/4, and each 180° is π rad, so 135° = 3π/4 ≈ 2.36 rad. Writing 3/4 drops the π."}],"explain":{"kid":["Take a piece of string as long as the radius of a circle. Lay it round the edge. The angle it makes at the centre is one radian.","One radian is about 57°. It is a big angle, not a tiny one like 1°.","The whole edge is 2πr long, so about 6.28 strings fit round. A full turn is 2π radians. Half a turn is π radians.","With radians, arc length is easy. An angle of 3 radians on a 4 cm circle has an arc of 3 × 4 = 12 cm."],"adult":["An angle in radians is the arc length divided by the radius, θ = s/r. It is a ratio of two lengths, so it has no unit and does not depend on the size of the circle.","The circumference is 2πr, so a full turn is 2πr ÷ r = 2π rad. That gives π rad = 180°. To change degrees to radians multiply by π/180; to change back multiply by 180/π. So 135° = 135/180 × π = 3π/4.","Rearranged, s = rθ. This is the degree formula θ/360 × 2πr with the 360 and the 2π already cancelled, which is why calculus and physics use radians.","Common mistakes: dropping the π (writing 135° as 3/4 rad); using the conversion factor upside down; putting degrees into s = rθ; thinking 1 rad is about 1°; and working out r ÷ s instead of s ÷ r."]},"familyTip":"Cut a string as long as the radius of a plate. Walk it round the rim, marking each length. How many fit? Then ask: would a bigger plate fit more strings, or the same number?","keyIdea":"One radian is the angle at the centre when the arc is as long as the radius, so an angle in radians is arc ÷ radius: θ = s/r. About 6.28 radius-lengths fit round a circle, so a full turn is 2π rad and 180° = π rad. In radians the arc length is simply s = rθ.","worked":{"title":"A circle has radius 8 cm. Find the arc for 135°, then the angle that gives an arc of 20 cm.","steps":["135° = 135/180 × π = 3π/4 rad.","s = rθ = 8 × 3π/4 = 6π ≈ 18.8 cm.","For an arc of 20 cm: θ = s ÷ r = 20 ÷ 8 = 2.5 rad.","In degrees: 2.5 × 180/π ≈ 143.2°. Check: 143.2/360 × 2π × 8 ≈ 20 cm."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which turn is each angle? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Half a turn","A quarter turn","A sixth of a turn"],"items":[["π rad",0],["180°",0],["π/2 rad",1],["90°",1],["π/3 rad",2],["60°",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrad","caption":"Flagship lab: lay radius-length strings round a circle, see one radian, a full turn of 2π, convert degrees to radians and find arcs with s = rθ.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0UJ58IxUYT":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A 4 cm side becomes 6 cm on a similar copy. What is the scale factor from the first shape to the copy?","choices":["1.5","2","0.67"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scale factor = new length ÷ old length.","explain":"6 ÷ 4 = 1.5. The difference 6 − 4 = 2 is not a scale factor, and 4 ÷ 6 goes the other way."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"DE is parallel to BC. AD = 3 cm, DB = 6 cm and DE = 4 cm. Find BC in cm.","answer":12,"hint":"AB = AD + DB. Compare AB with AD to get k.","explain":"AB = 9 cm, so k = 9 ÷ 3 = 3 and BC = 4 × 3 = 12 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ali says: '4 cm became 10 cm, so add 6. The 7 cm side becomes 13 cm.' What went wrong?","choices":["He added; similar shapes multiply, so it is 7 × 2.5 = 17.5 cm","He should have taken 6 away","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the copy made by adding to each side or by multiplying each side?","explain":"k = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5, so 7 × 2.5 = 17.5 cm. Adding 6 to every side changes the shape."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"DE is parallel to BC. AB = {s} cm, BC = {c} cm and DE = {d} cm. Find AD in cm.","answer":"s*d/c","hint":"Set up AD/AB = DE/BC, so AD/{s} = {d}/{c}.","explain":"AD = {s} × {d} ÷ {c} = {=s*d/c} cm. Keep small over big on both sides of the proportion.","params":{"s":[6,15],"c":[4,12],"d":[2,10]},"where":"(s*d)%c==0 && d<c"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"One triangle has sides 4, 6 and 8 cm. Another has sides 6, 8 and 10 cm. Are they similar?","choices":["No: 6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 but 10 ÷ 8 = 1.25","Yes: each side is 2 cm longer","Yes: they are both triangles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide each side of the second by the matching side of the first. Are the answers equal?","explain":"The ratios 1.5, 1.33 and 1.25 differ, so there is no single scale factor and the shapes are not similar."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A model car is similar to a real car that is 4 m long and 1.5 m tall. The model is 20 cm long. How tall is the model, in cm?","answer":7.5,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"Use the same units: 4 m = 400 cm. Then k = 20 ÷ 400.","explain":"k = 20 ÷ 400 = 0.05, and 1.5 m = 150 cm, so the model is 150 × 0.05 = 7.5 cm tall."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A similar copy of a triangle has been flipped and turned. How do you find the side that matches the 5 cm side?","choices":["Find the side opposite the same angle","Pick the side in the same place on the page","Pick the longest side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turning or flipping moves sides around but keeps every angle the same.","explain":"Matching sides sit opposite equal angles. Position on the page changes when a shape is turned, so it cannot be trusted."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"On the bigger of two similar shapes, a {=a*k} cm side matches a {a} cm side. Another side of the bigger shape is {=b*k} cm. How long is the matching side on the smaller shape?","answer":"b","hint":"Going from big to small, divide by k = {=a*k} ÷ {a}.","explain":"k = {k}, so the smaller side is {=b*k} ÷ {k} = {b} cm.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[3,8],"k":[2,5]},"where":"a!=b"}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Two triangles are similar. A {a} cm side matches a {=a*k} cm side. How long is the side that matches the {b} cm side?","answer":"b*k","hint":"Find the scale factor first: {=a*k} ÷ {a}. Then multiply {b} by it.","explain":"k = {=a*k} ÷ {a} = {k}, so the matching side is {b} × {k} = {=b*k} cm.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,9],"k":[2,4]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"DE is parallel to BC. AD = {p} cm, DB = {q} cm and DE = {d} cm. How long is BC?","answer":"d*(p+q)/p","hint":"AD matches the whole side AB = {p} + {q}, not DB.","explain":"AB = {=p+q} cm, so k = {=p+q} ÷ {p} and BC = {d} × {=p+q} ÷ {p} = {=d*(p+q)/p} cm.","params":{"p":[2,5],"q":[2,8],"d":[2,6]},"where":"(d*(p+q))%p==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A 6 cm side matches a 9 cm side on a similar shape. How long is the side that matches 8 cm?","choices":["12 cm","11 cm","5.3 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find k = 9 ÷ 6, then multiply 8 by it.","explain":"k = 1.5, so 8 × 1.5 = 12 cm. 11 cm adds 3 instead of multiplying, and 5.3 cm divides by k."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A photo 20 cm wide and 12 cm tall is shrunk to 5 cm wide without squashing it. How tall is it now, in cm?","answer":3,"hint":"The scale factor is new ÷ old = 5 ÷ 20. It is less than 1.","explain":"k = 5 ÷ 20 = 0.25, so the height is 12 × 0.25 = 3 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Triangle ADE sits inside triangle ABC, with DE parallel to BC. Which side of ABC matches AD?","choices":["AB","DB","BC"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pull ADE out. Which side of the big triangle starts at A and runs the same way as AD?","explain":"AD is part of AB, and both start at the shared angle A. DB is only the leftover piece, so it does not match."}],"explain":{"kid":["Two shapes are similar when one is a scaled copy of the other. The angles stay the same. Every side is multiplied by the same number, the scale factor k.","Find k from one pair of matching sides. If 4 cm becomes 10 cm, k = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5. Then a 6 cm side becomes 6 × 2.5 = 15 cm.","Do not add. Going from 4 to 10 adds 6, but adding 6 to every side would change the shape. Copies always multiply.","A copy can be turned or flipped. Match sides by the angles. Each side sits opposite an angle, and matching sides sit opposite equal angles."],"adult":["Similar figures have equal matching angles, and matching lengths share one ratio: the scale factor k = image length ÷ object length. A missing length is a known length × k. It is also the answer to a proportion such as x/6 = 10/4.","Pair the sides by angles, not by where they are drawn. In a triangle, the shortest side is opposite the smallest angle. Mark the equal angles and read the side opposite each one. This still works after a flip or a turn.","Overlapping triangles are the usual exam setting. If DE is parallel to BC, triangle ADE is similar to ABC. They share angle A, and the parallel lines make equal corresponding angles. Redraw them apart: AD pairs with the whole of AB = AD + DB, and DE pairs with BC.","Common mistakes: adding the same amount to every side instead of multiplying; pairing AD with DB rather than AB; matching sides by where they are drawn; dividing by k when going from small to large; and writing a proportion with one fraction upside down. A k below 1 still multiplies; it shrinks the shape."]},"familyTip":"Take a photo and print or show it at two sizes. Measure the width and height of each. Divide new by old for both. Are the two answers the same? Now try squashing the image in an editor: the two answers no longer match, and the picture looks wrong.","keyIdea":"Similar shapes are the same shape at different sizes, so every length is multiplied by one scale factor k = new length ÷ matching old length. Match sides by the equal angles they sit opposite, pull overlapping triangles apart first, then multiply by k or solve a proportion.","worked":{"title":"DE is parallel to BC. AD = 3 cm, DB = 5 cm and DE = 4.5 cm. Find BC.","steps":["Pull the triangles apart: ADE is similar to ABC, sharing the angle at A.","AD matches the whole of AB = 3 + 5 = 8 cm, not DB.","Scale factor from ADE to ABC: k = 8 ÷ 3.","BC = 4.5 × 8 ÷ 3 = 12 cm. Check: 3/8 = 4.5/12 = 0.375."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which triangles are similar to one with sides 4, 6 and 8 cm? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Similar to 4, 6, 8","Not similar"],"items":[["2, 3, 4",0],["8, 12, 16",0],["6, 9, 12",0],["6, 8, 10",1],["5, 7, 9",1],["4, 6, 9",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsim","caption":"Flagship lab: stretch a triangle by a scale factor, match sides by their angles, pull overlapping triangles apart, slide a parallel cut and shrink a photo.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_I1uIFFoUsb":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is opposite ÷ hypotenuse the same in every right triangle with a 35° angle?","choices":["They all have angles 35°, 90° and 55°, so they are similar","They all have the same hypotenuse","The opposite side is always 0.574 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do the angles of these triangles have in common?","explain":"Equal angles make the triangles similar. Similar triangles multiply every side by the same factor, so ratios of sides do not change."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"sin 35° ≈ 0.574. A right triangle with a 35° angle has hypotenuse 20 cm. How long is the side opposite the 35° angle, to 1 decimal place?","answer":11.5,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"opposite ÷ hypotenuse = 0.574, so opposite = 20 × 0.574.","explain":"20 × 0.574 = 11.48, which is 11.5 cm to 1 decimal place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Triangle A has opposite 3 and hypotenuse 5. Triangle B has the same angle θ and hypotenuse 15. Which is true?","choices":["B has opposite 9, and both have sin θ = 0.6","B has a bigger sin θ because it is bigger","B has opposite 13"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the scale factor 15 ÷ 5, then multiply.","explain":"k = 3, so B's opposite side is 9. Then 9 ÷ 15 = 3 ÷ 5 = 0.6. Size does not change sin θ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In triangle PQR the right angle is at R and θ is at Q. What is cos θ?","choices":["QR/PQ","PR/PQ","QR/PR"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hypotenuse: opposite the right angle. Adjacent: touches Q and is not the hypotenuse.","explain":"PQ is the hypotenuse and QR is adjacent to θ, so cos θ = QR/PQ. PR/PQ is sin θ."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A right triangle has opposite {=3*k}, adjacent {=4*k} and hypotenuse {=5*k}. What is sin θ as a decimal?","answer":"3*k/(5*k)","hint":"sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse = {=3*k} ÷ {=5*k}.","explain":"{=3*k} ÷ {=5*k} = 0.6, the same as 3 ÷ 5. The {k} cancels, whatever size the triangle is.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Kai says: 'sin 20° ≈ 0.342, so sin 40° ≈ 0.684.' Is he right?","choices":["No: sin 40° ≈ 0.643, because sine is not proportional to the angle","Yes: double the angle, double the sine","No: sin 40° is 0.342 as well"],"answer":0,"hint":"Draw both triangles with hypotenuse 10. Does the opposite side double?","explain":"sin 40° ≈ 0.643. The ratio grows with the angle, but more and more slowly, so doubling θ does not double sin θ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which of these can never be bigger than 1?","choices":["sin θ, since the hypotenuse is the longest side","tan θ, since it uses the two shorter sides","Neither of them can be bigger than 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which ratio has the longest side on the bottom?","explain":"sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse, and the hypotenuse is longest, so sin θ < 1. tan 60° ≈ 1.73, so tan can be bigger than 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Two right triangles share an angle θ. The small one has adjacent {a} cm and hypotenuse {h} cm. The big one has adjacent {=a*k} cm. How long is its hypotenuse?","answer":"h*k","hint":"Scale factor = {=a*k} ÷ {a}. Multiply the hypotenuse by it.","explain":"k = {k}, so the hypotenuse is {h} × {k} = {=h*k} cm, and cos θ stays {a}/{h}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"h":[7,12],"k":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Two right triangles share an angle θ. The small one has opposite {o} cm and hypotenuse {h} cm. The big one has hypotenuse {=h*k} cm. How long is its opposite side?","answer":"o*k","hint":"The triangles are similar. The scale factor is {=h*k} ÷ {h}.","explain":"k = {k}, so the opposite side is {o} × {k} = {=o*k} cm. The ratio opposite ÷ hypotenuse stays {o}/{h}.","params":{"o":[2,6],"h":[7,12],"k":[2,4]},"where":"o<h"},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A right triangle has a 40° angle. What is its third angle?","choices":["50°","40°","140°"],"answer":0,"hint":"The three angles add up to 180°, and one of them is 90°.","explain":"180 − 90 − 40 = 50°. Every right triangle with a 40° angle has angles 40°, 50° and 90°, so they are all similar."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In a right triangle, opposite = {o} and hypotenuse = {h}. A similar triangle is {k} times as big. What is opposite ÷ hypotenuse in the big triangle? Give a decimal.","answer":"o/h","hint":"Both sides are multiplied by {k}. What happens to the fraction?","explain":"({o} × {k}) ÷ ({h} × {k}) = {o} ÷ {h} = {=o/h}. The {k} cancels, so the ratio is unchanged.","params":{"o":[1,4],"h":[5,10],"k":[2,5]},"where":"(100*o)%h==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"In triangle PQR the right angle is at Q and θ is at P. Which side is opposite θ?","choices":["QR","PQ","PR"],"answer":0,"hint":"The opposite side is the one that does not touch the corner P.","explain":"QR does not touch P, so it is opposite θ. PR is opposite the right angle, so it is the hypotenuse, and PQ is adjacent."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"sin 30° = 0.5. What is sin 60°?","choices":["About 0.87","1","0.25"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is sin θ proportional to θ? Try it on a calculator.","explain":"sin 60° ≈ 0.866. Doubling the angle does not double the ratio, and sin θ can never be more than 1."}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw two right triangles with a 35° angle, one small and one big. Both have a third angle of 55°, because the angles add up to 180°.","Same angles means same shape. The big one is a scaled copy of the small one. Every side is multiplied by the same number.","So the fraction opposite ÷ hypotenuse is the same in both. If the big one is twice as big, the top and the bottom both double. 2.87 ÷ 5 and 5.74 ÷ 10 both give 0.574.","That number belongs to the angle, not to the triangle. We call it sin 35°. A calculator knows it for every angle."],"adult":["Two right triangles that share an acute angle θ have all three angles equal: θ, 90° and 90° − θ. So they are similar (AA). Matching sides share one scale factor k, and a ratio of two sides in one triangle does not change when both are multiplied by k.","That is why sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent are well defined. Each one depends on θ alone. A calculator's sin 35° ≈ 0.574 is that shared ratio, for a triangle of any size.","The names come from where θ is. The hypotenuse is opposite the right angle. The opposite side does not touch θ. The adjacent side touches θ and is not the hypotenuse. Turn the triangle and the names move with θ.","Common mistakes: thinking a bigger triangle has a bigger sine; treating sin θ as proportional to θ (sin 60° ≈ 0.866, not 2 × sin 30° = 1); calling the bottom side adjacent whichever way the triangle faces; writing a ratio upside down, which would make sin or cos bigger than 1; and reading 0.574 as a length instead of a ratio."]},"familyTip":"With a protractor, draw two right triangles that both have a 35° angle, one small and one as big as the page. Measure the side opposite the 35° angle and the longest side of each, then divide. Do both give about 0.57? Check sin 35 on a calculator.","keyIdea":"Every right triangle with the same acute angle θ has the same three angles, θ, 90° and 90° − θ, so they are all similar. Similar triangles keep their side ratios, so opposite ÷ hypotenuse, adjacent ÷ hypotenuse and opposite ÷ adjacent depend only on θ. Those fixed numbers are sin θ, cos θ and tan θ.","worked":{"title":"Show that sin 35° is the same in right triangles with hypotenuse 5 cm and 12 cm.","steps":["Both triangles have angles 35°, 90° and 55°, so they are similar.","Scale factor from the small to the big triangle: k = 12 ÷ 5 = 2.4.","The small opposite side is about 2.868 cm, so the big one is 2.868 × 2.4 ≈ 6.883 cm.","Ratios: 2.868 ÷ 5 ≈ 0.574 and 6.883 ÷ 12 ≈ 0.574. Both equal sin 35°."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which changes make sin θ change? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Changes sin θ","Leaves sin θ the same"],"items":[["Make θ bigger",0],["Make θ smaller",0],["Move θ to the other sharp corner",0],["Double every side",1],["Halve every side",1],["Flip the triangle over",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtrig","caption":"Flagship lab: resize a 35° right triangle, cut similar copies from it, name the sides of a turned triangle and swing the angle to see what sin θ depends on.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zepH6Dsn_G":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is sin 45° in exact form?","choices":["√2/2","1/2","√2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Half a unit square has legs 1 and 1 and hypotenuse √2.","explain":"sin 45° = 1/√2 = √2/2 ≈ 0.707. 1/2 treats 45° as half of 90° in value, and √2 is hypotenuse ÷ leg."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is tan 90°?","choices":["1","0","Undefined: cos 90° = 0, so it would divide by 0"],"answer":2,"hint":"tan = sin ÷ cos. What is cos 90°?","explain":"At 90° the adjacent side shrinks to 0, so tan 90° = 1 ÷ 0, which has no value. tan 45° is the one equal to 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A right triangle has hypotenuse {d} cm and an angle of 60°. How long is the side next to the 60° angle (not the hypotenuse), in cm?","answer":"d/2","hint":"The side next to 60° is adjacent, so use cos 60° = 1/2.","explain":"Adjacent = {d} × cos 60° = {d} × 1/2 = {=d/2} cm.","params":{"d":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which triangle shows that sin 60° = √3/2?","choices":["Half of an equilateral triangle with side 2","Half of a square with side 1","A 3, 4, 5 triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need angles of 30°, 60° and 90°.","explain":"Halving an equilateral triangle of side 2 gives sides 1, √3, 2 and angles 30°, 60°, 90°. Half a square gives 45°, and a 3, 4, 5 triangle has no 60° angle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ana says sin 60° = 2 × sin 30° = 1. What went wrong?","choices":["Sine is not proportional to the angle: sin 60° = √3/2 ≈ 0.87","She should have used 3 × sin 30°","Nothing, sin 60° is 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Doubling the angle does not double the side opposite it.","explain":"sin 60° = √3/2 ≈ 0.866, not 1. Only sin 90° = 1. The sine curve bends, so it is not a straight line through the origin."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Without a calculator, work out sin 30° + cos 60°.","answer":1,"tolerance":0.001,"hint":"Both come from the half equilateral triangle. Which side is opposite 30° and which is adjacent to 60°?","explain":"sin 30° = 1/2 and cos 60° = 1/2, the same side 1 over the hypotenuse 2. So the sum is 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Half an equilateral triangle has hypotenuse 2 and base 1. What is its exact height?","choices":["√3","1","√5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pythagoras: height² = 2² − 1².","explain":"height² = 4 − 1 = 3, so the height is √3 ≈ 1.73. 1 comes from 2 − 1 without squaring, and √5 adds the squares instead of subtracting."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Work out (sin 60°)² without a calculator. Give your answer as a decimal.","answer":0.75,"tolerance":0.001,"hint":"sin 60° = √3/2. Square the top and the bottom.","explain":"(√3/2)² = 3/4 = 0.75. Check: cos 60° = 1/2, and 3/4 + 1/4 = 1, as sin² + cos² = 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What is cos 0°?","choices":["0","1","Undefined"],"answer":1,"hint":"Picture a hypotenuse of 1 lying flat on the ground. How long is the base?","explain":"At 0° the hypotenuse lies along the ground, so the base is the whole length 1 and the height is 0: cos 0° = 1 and sin 0° = 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A straight ramp rises at 30° to the ground and its top is {k} m high. How long is the ramp, in metres?","answer":"2*k","hint":"The height is opposite 30°, so height = ramp × sin 30° = ramp × 1/2. Work backwards.","explain":"{k} = ramp × 1/2, so the ramp is 2 × {k} = {=2*k} m. Halving the height instead would make the ramp shorter than its own height.","params":{"k":[1,12]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A ladder {d} m long leans at 30° to the ground. How high up the wall does it reach, in metres?","answer":"d/2","hint":"The height is opposite the 30° angle, so height = {d} × sin 30°, and sin 30° = 1/2.","explain":"Height = {d} × sin 30° = {d} × 1/2 = {=d/2} m.","params":{"d":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What is the exact value of cos 30°?","choices":["√3/2","1/2","√3"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the half triangle with sides 1, √3, 2, which side touches the 30° corner but is not the hypotenuse?","explain":"The √3 side is adjacent to 30°, and the hypotenuse is 2, so cos 30° = √3/2. 1/2 is sin 30°, and √3 drops the ÷ 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A right triangle has a 45° angle. The side opposite it is {k} cm. How long is the side adjacent to it, in cm?","answer":"k","hint":"tan 45° = opposite ÷ adjacent = 1, so the two shorter sides are equal.","explain":"tan 45° = 1, so adjacent = opposite = {k} cm. It is half of a square.","params":{"k":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is the exact value of tan 30°?","choices":["√3","√3/3","1/2"],"answer":1,"hint":"tan = opposite ÷ adjacent. Opposite 30° is 1 and adjacent is √3.","explain":"tan 30° = 1/√3. Multiply top and bottom by √3 to get √3/3 ≈ 0.577. √3 is tan 60°, and 1/2 is sin 30°."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A right triangle has hypotenuse 10 cm and an angle of 60°. What is the exact length of the side opposite 60°?","choices":["5√3 cm","5 cm","10√3 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Opposite = hypotenuse × sin 60°, and sin 60° = √3/2.","explain":"10 × √3/2 = 5√3 ≈ 8.66 cm. 5 cm is the side next to 60° (10 × cos 60°), and 10√3 is longer than the hypotenuse."}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw a triangle with all three sides 2. Fold it in half from the top. Each half has angles of 30°, 60° and 90°.","The bottom of each half is 1. The long side is still 2. To find the height, square and take away: 2 × 2 − 1 × 1 = 3. So the height is √3.","Now read the sides. Sin of an angle is the side across from it over the long side. Across from 30° is 1, so sin 30° is 1/2. Across from 60° is √3, so sin 60° is √3/2.","Cut a square of side 1 from corner to corner. Both small angles are 45°, and the cut is √2 long. Tan 45° is 1 ÷ 1, which is 1."],"adult":["Halve an equilateral triangle of side 2. The half has hypotenuse 2 and short side 1. By Pythagoras its height is √(4 − 1) = √3. The 30° angle faces the side 1, so sin 30° = 1/2, cos 30° = √3/2 and tan 30° = 1/√3 = √3/3.","The 60° angle swaps opposite and adjacent, so sin 60° = √3/2, cos 60° = 1/2 and tan 60° = √3. Cutting a unit square on its diagonal gives legs 1 and 1 and hypotenuse √2. So sin 45° = cos 45° = 1/√2 = √2/2, and tan 45° = 1.","At the ends, a hypotenuse of 1 lies flat at 0° and stands up at 90°. So sin 0° = 0, cos 0° = 1, tan 0° = 0, sin 90° = 1 and cos 90° = 0. tan 90° is undefined, because it would mean dividing by 0. Exact values keep answers exact: a 6 m ladder at 60° reaches 6 × √3/2 = 3√3 m, not a rounded 5.196 m. A memory aid: the sines of 0°, 30°, 45°, 60° and 90° are √0/2, √1/2, √2/2, √3/2 and √4/2. The cosines are the same list in reverse.","Common mistakes: thinking sin 60° is double sin 30°; swapping sin and cos for 30° and 60°; writing tan 30° as √3, which is tan 60°; dropping the ÷ 2 so that sin 60° becomes √3; finding the height as 2 − 1 instead of √(2² − 1²); and giving tan 90° a value."]},"familyTip":"Cut a paper square in half along its diagonal and fold a paper equilateral triangle in half. Label the sides 1, 1, √2 and 1, √3, 2. Then quiz each other: which side is across from 30°? What is sin 30°?","keyIdea":"Two special triangles give exact values with no calculator. Halve an equilateral triangle of side 2 to get sides 1, √3 and 2 with angles 30°, 60° and 90°. Cut a unit square along its diagonal to get sides 1, 1 and √2 with two 45° angles. Read sin, cos and tan off the sides: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3.","worked":{"title":"A ramp is 8 m long and rises at 30° to the ground. Find its exact height and the exact distance it covers along the ground.","steps":["The ramp is the hypotenuse, 8 m. The height is opposite 30°, so height = 8 × sin 30°.","sin 30° = 1/2 from the half equilateral triangle, so height = 8 × 1/2 = 4 m.","The ground distance is adjacent to 30°, so it is 8 × cos 30° = 8 × √3/2 = 4√3 m.","Check with Pythagoras: 4² + (4√3)² = 16 + 48 = 64 = 8². 4√3 ≈ 6.93 m."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which exact value is each one? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["1/2","√2/2","√3/2"],"items":[["sin 30°",0],["cos 60°",0],["sin 45°",1],["cos 45°",1],["sin 60°",2],["cos 30°",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagexact","caption":"Flagship lab: fold an equilateral triangle and cut a square to find exact sin, cos and tan, then use them for surd lengths.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qaf61_4L_L":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does sin²θ mean?","choices":["(sin θ)²","sin(θ²)","2 sin θ"],"answer":0,"hint":"The small 2 belongs to the whole ratio.","explain":"sin²θ is shorthand for (sin θ)², the sine of θ multiplied by itself."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"cos θ = 0.6. Which is true?","choices":["sin θ = 0.8 or −0.8, depending on the quadrant","sin θ = 0.4","sin θ = 0.8 in every quadrant"],"answer":0,"hint":"sin²θ = 1 − 0.36. How many square roots does 0.64 have?","explain":"sin²θ = 0.64, so sin θ = ±0.8. Above the x-axis it is 0.8; below it is −0.8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Simplify (sin θ + cos θ)².","choices":["1 + 2 sin θ cos θ","1","sin θ + cos θ"],"answer":0,"hint":"Expand the bracket before using the identity.","explain":"(sin θ + cos θ)² = sin²θ + 2 sin θ cos θ + cos²θ = 1 + 2 sin θ cos θ."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"cos θ = 1/{k}. Find tan²θ + 1.","answer":"k*k","params":{"k":[2,9]},"hint":"Divide sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 by cos²θ.","explain":"tan²θ + 1 = 1/cos²θ = 1 ÷ (1/{k})² = {=k*k}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"sin θ = −8/17 and θ is in quadrant IV. What is cos θ?","choices":["15/17","−15/17","9/17"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos²θ = 1 − 64/289. In quadrant IV, is x positive or negative?","explain":"cos²θ = 225/289, so cos θ = ±15/17. In quadrant IV, x is positive, so cos θ = 15/17."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Find {k} − {k} sin²({a}°) − {k} cos²({a}°).","answer":"0*k","params":{"k":[2,9],"a":[1,359]},"hint":"Group the two squared terms and take out {k}.","explain":"{k} − {k}(sin²({a}°) + cos²({a}°)) = {k} − {k} × 1 = {=0*k}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A student writes: sin θ = 0.6, so cos θ = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4. What went wrong?","choices":["The identity is about squares: cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ","Nothing; the working is right","They should have added: cos θ = 1.6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the identity sin θ + cos θ = 1?","explain":"sin θ + cos θ is not 1. Use squares: cos²θ = 1 − 0.36 = 0.64, so cos θ = ±0.8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"cos θ = {c}/10 with θ in quadrant I. Find sin²θ as a decimal.","answer":"(100-c*c)/100","params":{"c":[1,9]},"hint":"sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ. Square {c}/10 first.","explain":"sin²θ = 1 − ({c}/10)² = 1 − {=c*c/100} = {=(100-c*c)/100}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Rewrite 2 sin²x + 3 cos x = 3 using only cos x.","choices":["2 − 2cos²x + 3 cos x = 3","2 + 3 cos x = 3","2 sin x + 3 cos x = 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Replace sin²x with 1 − cos²x.","explain":"sin²x = 1 − cos²x, so 2 sin²x = 2 − 2cos²x, giving 2 − 2cos²x + 3 cos x = 3, a quadratic in cos x."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why is sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 for every angle θ?","choices":["The point (cos θ, sin θ) on the unit circle is 1 from the origin","sin θ and cos θ are always equal to 0.5","It is only true when θ is acute"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is the point (cos θ, sin θ), and how far is it from the centre?","explain":"The point lies on x² + y² = 1, so its coordinates satisfy cos²θ + sin²θ = 1. That is true in every quadrant."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"sin θ = {s}/10. Find cos²θ as a decimal.","answer":"(100-s*s)/100","params":{"s":[1,9]},"hint":"Use cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ. Square sin θ first.","explain":"cos²θ = 1 − ({s}/10)² = 1 − {=s*s/100} = {=(100-s*s)/100}."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"sin θ = 3/5 and θ is in quadrant II. What is cos θ?","choices":["−4/5","4/5","2/5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find cos²θ, then use the quadrant to choose the sign.","explain":"cos²θ = 1 − 9/25 = 16/25, so cos θ = ±4/5. In quadrant II, x is negative, so cos θ = −4/5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Without a calculator, find {k} sin²({a}°) + {k} cos²({a}°).","answer":"k","params":{"k":[2,9],"a":[1,359]},"hint":"Take out the common factor first.","explain":"{k}(sin²({a}°) + cos²({a}°)) = {k} × 1 = {=k}, whatever the angle."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Simplify (1 − cos²θ) / sin θ.","choices":["sin θ","1","cos θ"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rewrite 1 − cos²θ using the identity.","explain":"1 − cos²θ = sin²θ, and sin²θ / sin θ = sin θ."}],"explain":{"kid":["Take a circle with radius 1. Turn a radius by an angle θ. Its end point is at (cos θ, sin θ). Drop a line straight down from that point. Now you have a right triangle. Its long side is 1. Its short sides are cos θ across and sin θ up.","Pythagoras says the two short sides, squared, add up to the long side squared. So cos²θ + sin²θ = 1² = 1. Here sin²θ means sin θ times sin θ. It is not sin of θ squared.","Turn the radius past 90°. Now cos θ or sin θ can be less than zero. But a square is never less than zero, and the point is still 1 from the centre. So the rule holds for every angle you pick.","The rule helps you find one ratio from the other. Say sin θ = 0.6. Then cos²θ = 1 − 0.36 = 0.64, so cos θ is 0.8 or −0.8. The quadrant tells you which one. In quadrant II, cos θ is −0.8. The rule also tidies up messy sums. Where you see 1 − cos²θ, write sin²θ. Where you see sin²θ + cos²θ, write 1."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 16 to 18, proves and uses the Pythagorean identity sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. The proof is short. On the unit circle the point at angle θ is (cos θ, sin θ). It lies on x² + y² = 1, so cos²θ + sin²θ = 1. For an acute angle this is Pythagoras in the triangle with hypotenuse 1. For other angles the signs change but the squares do not, so it holds for all θ.","Rearranged, it gives sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ and cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ. Taking a square root gives two values, so the quadrant must pick the sign. If sin θ = 5/13 and θ is obtuse, then cos θ = −12/13. Dividing the identity by cos²θ gives tan²θ + 1 = 1/cos²θ, a useful second form.","In simplifying, spot the pattern and swap. (1 − cos²θ)/sin θ becomes sin²θ/sin θ = sin θ. (sin θ + cos θ)² expands to 1 + 2 sin θ cos θ. In equations, the identity turns a mix of sines and cosines into one function, such as a quadratic in cos θ.","Common mistakes: reading sin²θ as sin(θ²); taking cos θ = 1 − sin θ instead of using the squares; dropping the negative root, so the quadrant is ignored; and writing (sin θ + cos θ)² as sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. A quick check is to substitute an angle such as 40° into both sides."]},"familyTip":"Pick any angle and type sin(θ)² + cos(θ)² into a calculator. Try 17°, 140° and 300°. Then ask why the answer never changes, and what that has to do with a circle of radius 1.","keyIdea":"For every angle θ, sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. It is Pythagoras on the unit circle: the point (cos θ, sin θ) is 1 from the origin. Rearranged as cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ, it turns one ratio into the other, with the quadrant fixing the sign, and it simplifies expressions.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sort each expression by what it simplifies to, using sin²θ + cos²θ = 1.","opts":{"bins":["cos²θ","sin²θ","1"],"items":[["1 − sin²θ",0],["(1 − sin θ)(1 + sin θ)",0],["1 − cos²θ",1],["(1 − cos θ)(1 + cos θ)",1],["sin²θ + cos²θ",2],["cos²50° + sin²50°",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpyid","caption":"Flagship lab: turn the unit-circle point and watch the squares on its legs always fill 1, then pick signs by quadrant and simplify.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oM7XSZb9pI":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the pole in polar coordinates?","choices":["The fixed point that r is measured from","The point where θ = π/2","The end of the initial line"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do you start before you turn and walk?","explain":"The pole O is the fixed starting point. r is the distance from it, and the initial line starts there."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which polar point is the same as (−{a}, π/6)?","choices":["({a}, 7π/6)","({a}, π/6)","({a}, −π/6)"],"answer":0,"hint":"A negative r walks backwards. Which turn faces that way?","explain":"(−{a}, π/6) faces π/6 and walks back {a}, which is the same as turning an extra π: ({a}, 7π/6).","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The polar point ({a}, 3π/2) has which Cartesian y-coordinate?","answer":"-a","hint":"3π/2 is three quarters of a turn. Which way does that point?","explain":"y = {a} × sin(3π/2) = {a} × (−1) = −{a}. Three quarters of a turn points straight down.","params":{"a":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why does a point have many polar names but only one Cartesian name?","choices":["Turning a full 2π more lands on the same ray","Because r can be any size","Because radians are not exact"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens if you turn one extra full lap?","explain":"(r, θ) and (r, θ + 2π) are the same point, so you can add or take away any number of full turns."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which polar point is the Cartesian point (0, {a})?","choices":["({a}, π/2)","({a}, 0)","(0, {a})"],"answer":0,"hint":"The point is straight up, {a} from the pole.","explain":"Straight up is a quarter turn, π/2, and the distance is {a}, so ({a}, π/2). (0, {a}) in polar would be the pole.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam plots (2, π/3) by turning π/3 clockwise from straight up. What went wrong?","choices":["He used bearings: θ is anticlockwise from the initial line","Nothing, that is correct","He should have walked 3 units"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does θ start, and which way does it turn?","explain":"Polar angles start on the initial line, pointing right, and turn anticlockwise. Sam measured like a bearing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which polar coordinates describe the pole?","choices":["(0, θ) for any θ","Only (0, 0)","(1, 0)"],"answer":0,"hint":"If r = 0, does the turn matter?","explain":"With r = 0 you never walk away from O, so every angle gives the pole."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Is (4, π/4 + π) the same point as (4, π/4)?","choices":["No, it is on the opposite side of the pole","Yes, adding π is a full turn","Yes, because r is the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"A full turn is 2π, not π.","explain":"Adding π is a half turn, so the ray points the opposite way. Only 2π comes back to the same point."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In polar coordinates, where is θ measured from, and which way?","choices":["From the initial line, anticlockwise","From straight up, clockwise","From the initial line, clockwise"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the ray that points right from the pole.","explain":"θ starts on the initial line and positive θ turns anticlockwise. Clockwise from straight up is how bearings work, not polar angles."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The polar point ({a}, π) has which Cartesian x-coordinate?","answer":"-a","hint":"Use x = r cos θ, and cos π = −1.","explain":"x = {a} × cos π = {a} × (−1) = −{a}. A half turn points left of the pole.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is another name for the point ({a}, π/3)?","choices":["({a}, 7π/3)","({a}, 4π/3)","({a}, −π/3)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which change is a full turn?","explain":"7π/3 = π/3 + 2π, a full extra lap, so ({a}, 7π/3) is the same point. Adding π points the other way, and −π/3 is the mirror image.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The polar point ({a}, π/2) has which Cartesian y-coordinate?","answer":"a","hint":"Use y = r sin θ, and sin(π/2) = 1.","explain":"y = {a} × sin(π/2) = {a}. A quarter turn points straight up, so the point is {a} units above the pole.","params":{"a":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Where is the point (3, 2π/3)?","choices":["Up and to the left of the pole","Up and to the right of the pole","Down and to the left of the pole"],"answer":0,"hint":"π/2 is straight up. Is 2π/3 more or less than π/2?","explain":"2π/3 is more than π/2 but less than π, so the ray points up and to the left."}],"explain":{"kid":["Polar coordinates find a point by a turn and a walk. Start at the pole, O. Face along the initial line, which points right. Turn by θ, then walk r units out. So (3, π/2) means a quarter turn, then 3 steps: straight up.","θ is in radians and it turns anticlockwise. A half turn is π and a full turn is 2π. So 2π/3 is past straight up: the point (3, 2π/3) sits up and to the left. A negative θ, like −π/2, turns clockwise.","One point has many names. A full extra lap brings you back to the same ray. So (3, π/4), (3, 9π/4) and (3, −7π/4) are all one point. The pole is (0, θ) for any θ, since you never walk away.","If r is negative, face along θ but walk backwards. (−2, π/3) lands on the far side of the pole, at the same place as (2, 4π/3). To get x and y, use x = r cos θ and y = r sin θ. So (4, π) is the Cartesian point (−4, 0)."],"adult":["This A-level Further Maths topic (ages 16 to 18) sets up the polar system: a fixed point O called the pole, a ray from it called the initial line, and points given as (r, θ). The angle is measured from the initial line, anticlockwise for positive θ, and in radians. The conversions are x = r cos θ, y = r sin θ and r² = x² + y².","Unlike Cartesian coordinates, polar names are not unique. (r, θ) and (r, θ + 2nπ) are the same point for any whole number n, and the pole is (0, θ) for every θ. If negative r is allowed, (−r, θ) is the same point as (r, θ + π). Exam boards often fix a principal form, such as r ≥ 0 with −π < θ ≤ π or 0 ≤ θ < 2π, so learners should check which their course uses.","Common mistakes: turning clockwise, as with bearings; measuring from the vertical instead of the initial line; reading 2π/3 as less than a right angle because it is a small number; and thinking (r, θ + π) is the same point, when it is opposite. Another slip is finding θ with tan⁻¹(y/x) and forgetting to check the quadrant.","In the lab, learners drag a point round a polar grid, plot points in three quadrants, wind extra laps to find three names for one point, walk backwards for negative r, and read x = r cos θ and y = r sin θ live. The challenge mixes plotting with naming the same point another way, with feedback on each slip."]},"familyTip":"Stand in a room facing one wall: that is the initial line. Call out a turn and a number of steps, such as \"a quarter turn left, then 3 steps\", and see where you end up. Then find another turn that ends in the same place.","keyIdea":"A polar point (r, θ) is found by turning from the initial line by θ radians anticlockwise, then walking r units out from the pole. Adding or taking away 2π gives the same point, so every point has many polar names. A negative r means walk backwards, which is the same as turning an extra π. To change to Cartesian, use x = r cos θ and y = r sin θ.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Same point as (2, π/3), or a different point? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Same point","Different point"],"items":[["(2, 7π/3)",0],["(2, −5π/3)",0],["(−2, 4π/3)",0],["(2, 4π/3)",1],["(2, −π/3)",1],["(−2, π/3)",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpolar","caption":"Flagship lab: turn and walk to plot polar points, wind extra laps for new names, walk backwards for negative r and read off x and y.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zt0LlV_JjB":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which shape has 3 corners?","choices":["Triangle","Square","Circle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the pointy bits on each shape.","explain":"A triangle has 3 corners and 3 straight sides. A square has 4 corners and a circle has none."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The square will not go in the round hole. Why?","choices":["It has corners","It is too red","It is upside down"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a square have that a round hole does not?","explain":"The square has corners and straight sides. The round hole has no corners, so the square needs the square hole."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which shape fits any way you turn it?","choices":["Circle","Triangle","Rectangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which shape looks the same however you turn it?","explain":"A circle fits any way round. It is round all the way, so there are no corners to line up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A long rectangle is standing up. Its hole lies flat. What do you do?","choices":["Turn it to lie flat","Push it in standing up","Put it in the square hole"],"answer":0,"hint":"Make the piece look like the hole.","explain":"Turn the rectangle so it lies flat like the hole. The square hole is too short for it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Leo fits {n} round pieces in a row. Which hole did they go in?","choices":["The round hole","The star hole","The square hole"],"answer":0,"hint":"Round pieces match a hole with the same round edge.","explain":"All {n} round pieces go in the round hole. Round fits round.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which one has no corners?","choices":["An oval","A square","A triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Run your finger round the edge. Do you feel a pointy bit?","explain":"An oval is curved all the way round, like an egg, so it has no corners."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia puts {n} blocks on a slope. They do not roll. Why not?","choices":["They have flat sides","They are too small","The slope is round"],"answer":0,"hint":"What stops a block? Look at its sides and corners.","explain":"All {n} blocks have flat sides and corners, so they slide or stop. Round things roll.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which hole does a round circle fit?","choices":["The round hole","The square hole","The triangle hole"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the hole with no pointy corners.","explain":"A circle fits the round hole. It has no corners, so it needs a hole with no corners."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ava has {n} triangles. One will not go in its hole. What should she do?","choices":["Turn it","Push harder","Throw it away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it the right shape? Then the corners must line up.","explain":"Turn it until the corners line up with the hole. All {n} triangles fit once they are turned the right way.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many corners does a square have?","choices":["4","3","None"],"answer":0,"hint":"Touch each pointy bit and count.","explain":"A square has 4 corners and 4 straight sides. A triangle has 3 corners, and a circle has none."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam puts {n} shapes on a slope. Which one rolls away?","choices":["The ball","The block","The triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which shape is round all the way, with no flat side to stop it?","explain":"The ball rolls. Of Sam's {n} shapes, only the round one has no flat side or corner to stop it.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at the shape. Now look at the holes. Find the hole that looks the same. Round goes in round. Square goes in square.","Bump! The triangle will not go in. Do not push. Turn it round and round. When the corners line up, it drops in. Plop!","A corner is a pointy bit. A square has 4 corners. A triangle has 3. A circle has none. It is round all the way.","Round things roll. Put a ball on a slope and it rolls away. A block has flat sides, so it stops."],"adult":["Fitting pieces into a shape sorter or inset puzzle is one of the first geometry tasks for two and three year olds. To do it, a child must match the outline of a piece to the outline of a hole, notice straight edges, corners and curves, and turn the piece until it lines up. This early spatial thinking supports later work on naming 2D shapes and their properties.","At first most toddlers use trial and error: they push a piece at every hole. With practice they start to look before they try, pick the matching hole, and rotate the piece in the hand before it gets there. You can help by naming what they feel: straight side, pointy corner, round edge.","Common mistakes to watch for: pushing harder instead of turning the piece; trying a square in the rectangle hole because both have four corners; and thinking a turned triangle is a different shape. Show that it is the same triangle, just turned. A circle fits any way round, a square fits four ways, and a triangle three ways, because of how their corners repeat.","In the lab your child drags a circle, a square and a triangle into a sorter, turns an upside-down triangle until it drops in, turns a standing rectangle so it lies flat, lets shapes go on a slope to see which one rolls, sorts shapes into corners and no corners, and fills the sorter with turned pieces."]},"familyTip":"Use a real shape sorter or cut shapes from a cereal box and draw round them to make holes. When a piece will not go in, say \"Let's turn it\" rather than doing it for them. At bath time, ask which toys roll and which slide.","keyIdea":"Each shape fits the hole that matches it: round in round, square in square, triangle in triangle. If a shape is the right one but will not go in, turn it until its straight sides and corners line up with the hole. A circle has no corners, so it fits any way round, and it is the shape that rolls.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Corners or no corners? Drag each shape, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📐 Has corners","⭕ No corners"],"items":[["Square",0],["Triangle",0],["Rectangle",0],["Diamond",0],["Circle",1],["Oval",1],["Ball",1],["Plate",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfit","caption":"Flagship lab: match shapes to holes, turn pieces until they fit, roll shapes down a slope and sort corners from curves.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qRlLrCnazx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"cos(A − B) = ?","choices":["cos A cos B + sin A sin B","cos A cos B − sin A sin B","cos A − cos B"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cosine flips the sign of the angle operation.","explain":"cos(A − B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B. With cos(A + B) the middle sign is minus; with A − B it becomes plus."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Expand cos({k}° − x).","choices":["cos {k}° cos x + sin {k}° sin x","cos {k}° cos x − sin {k}° sin x","cos {k}° − cos x"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos(A − B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B, with A = {k}° and B = x.","explain":"cos({k}° − x) = cos {k}° cos x + sin {k}° sin x. For cosine the sign flips: minus inside gives plus in the middle.","params":{"k":[10,80]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Solve sin 2x = cos x for 0° < x < 90°.","choices":["x = 30°","x = 45°","x = 60°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write sin 2x = 2 sin x cos x and factorise; do not divide by cos x blindly.","explain":"2 sin x cos x − cos x = cos x(2 sin x − 1) = 0. In 0° < x < 90°, cos x ≠ 0, so sin x = ½ and x = 30°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Simplify 2 sin {t}° cos {t}°.","choices":["sin {=2*t}°","2 sin {t}°","cos {=2*t}°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A backwards.","explain":"2 sin {t}° cos {t}° = sin(2 × {t}°) = sin {=2*t}°. Read sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A backwards.","params":{"t":[5,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is NOT equal to cos 2A?","choices":["2 cos A","1 − 2sin²A","cos²A − sin²A"],"answer":0,"hint":"Test A = 60°: cos 120° = −½.","explain":"2 cos 60° = 1, but cos 120° = −½, so 2 cos A is wrong. The other two are true forms of cos 2A."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"tan(A + B) = ?","choices":["(tan A + tan B)/(1 − tan A tan B)","(tan A + tan B)/(1 + tan A tan B)","tan A + tan B"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide sin(A + B) by cos(A + B), then divide top and bottom by cos A cos B.","explain":"The bottom comes from cos(A + B), which has a minus, so tan(A + B) = (tan A + tan B)/(1 − tan A tan B)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Prove-style step: cos⁴x − sin⁴x simplifies to?","choices":["cos 2x","1","cos 4x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Difference of two squares, then sin²x + cos²x = 1.","explain":"cos⁴x − sin⁴x = (cos²x − sin²x)(cos²x + sin²x) = (cos 2x)(1) = cos 2x."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Expand sin(x + {k}°).","choices":["sin x cos {k}° + cos x sin {k}°","sin x + sin {k}°","sin x cos {k}° − cos x sin {k}°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B with A = x.","explain":"sin(x + {k}°) = sin x cos {k}° + cos x sin {k}°. Sine does not split over the plus, and the sign stays + for sine.","params":{"k":[10,80]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the exact value of sin 15°.","choices":["(√6 − √2)/4","(√6 + √2)/4","√3/2 − √2/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write 15° = 45° − 30° and use sin(A − B).","explain":"sin 15° = sin 45° cos 30° − cos 45° sin 30° = (√2/2)(√3/2) − (√2/2)(½) = (√6 − √2)/4."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Write sin {=2*t}° as a product of sin {t}° and cos {t}°.","choices":["2 sin {t}° cos {t}°","2 sin {t}°","sin {t}° cos {t}°"],"answer":0,"hint":"sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A, with A = {t}°.","explain":"sin {=2*t}° = sin(2 × {t}°) = 2 sin {t}° cos {t}°. Doubling the angle does not double the sine, and the factor 2 is needed.","params":{"t":[10,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which form of cos 2x turns 3 cos 2x + 5 cos x = 1 into a quadratic in cos x?","choices":["2cos²x − 1","1 − 2sin²x","cos²x − sin²x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick the form that leaves only cos x.","explain":"2cos²x − 1 gives 6cos²x + 5cos x − 4 = 0, a quadratic in cos x. The other forms leave sin x in the equation."}],"explain":{"kid":["It is tempting to think sin(A + B) = sin A + sin B. Test it: A = 30° and B = 60° give sin 90° = 1. But sin 30° + sin 60° is about 1.37. No sine can be more than 1, so the rule is false.","The true rule is sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B. On a unit circle, the height of the point at angle A + B splits into two parts. One part is sin A cos B. The other is cos A sin B. They stack to the full height.","Cosine flips the sign: cos(A + B) = cos A cos B − sin A sin B. For A − B, swap B for −B. Since cos(−B) = cos B and sin(−B) = −sin B, each sign in the middle changes. These rules give exact values, such as sin 75° = sin(45° + 30°) = (√6 + √2)/4.","Set B = A to get double angles. sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A, not 2 sin A. cos 2A = cos²A − sin²A. Swap with sin²A + cos²A = 1 to get 2cos²A − 1 or 1 − 2sin²A. Pick the form that leaves one trig function in your equation."],"adult":["These formulae sit in A-level Pure Mathematics (Edexcel, AQA and OCR, topic Trigonometry). Learners must know and use sin(A ± B), cos(A ± B) and tan(A ± B), find exact values, and use the double-angle forms to solve equations and prove identities. The formulae are in the formula book, but the uses are not.","The proof can be seen on a unit circle. Drop the point at angle A + B onto the ray at angle A. The height then splits into sin A cos B and cos A sin B. The minus sign in cos(A + B) comes from the second piece: it points back towards the y-axis. The rest follows by putting −B for B, or by dividing sin by cos for tan.","Common mistakes: splitting the function over the plus (sin(A + B) = sin A + sin B, sin 2A = 2 sin A); using a plus in cos(A + B); writing B − A in place of A − B; giving a decimal when an exact surd is asked for; picking the wrong form of cos 2A, so two trig functions remain; and dividing both sides by sin x, which loses the solutions where sin x = 0. A careful learner always factorises instead.","The lab starts with the tempting rule on a unit circle, then has the learner stack the two pieces that make the height of the A + B point. Next they fix the signs of three formulae with a live numeric check, build exact values for 15°, 75° and 105°, and pick every form of cos 2A beside a graph of sin 2A and 2 sin A."]},"keyIdea":"Sine and cosine do not split over addition: sin(A + B) is not sin A + sin B. The compound-angle formulae are sin(A ± B) = sin A cos B ± cos A sin B, cos(A ± B) = cos A cos B ∓ sin A sin B and tan(A ± B) = (tan A ± tan B)/(1 ∓ tan A tan B). Putting B = A gives the double-angle formulae: sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A, cos 2A = cos²A − sin²A = 2cos²A − 1 = 1 − 2sin²A, and tan 2A = 2 tan A/(1 − tan²A).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each statement a true identity or a tempting myth? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ True identity","❌ Myth"],"items":[["sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A",0],["sin 2A = 2 sin A",1],["cos(A + B) = cos A cos B − sin A sin B",0],["sin(A + B) = sin A + sin B",1],["cos 2A = 1 − 2sin²A",0],["cos(A − B) = cos A − cos B",1],["sin(A − B) = sin A cos B − cos A sin B",0],["cos 2A = 2 cos A",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcomp","caption":"Flagship lab: test the tempting rule on a unit circle, stack the height of the A + B point, fix the signs, build exact values and pick every form of cos 2A.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Ap5JD4y89P":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why does a ball roll off a tower?","choices":["It is round all over","It is too heavy","It is too small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the outside of a ball. Is any part flat?","explain":"A ball has no flat face. It is round all over, so it rolls off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where can a cone go on a tower?","choices":["Only at the very top","At the bottom","In the middle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a block sit on a point?","explain":"A cone has a point on top, so nothing can sit on it. It can only be the top."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A block hangs far over the edge of the one below. What happens?","choices":["It tips off","It stays still","It gets bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the middle of the block still over the block below?","explain":"If its middle goes past the edge, it tips off. Line it up over the block below."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The gap is 3 blocks wide. Which plank makes a bridge?","choices":["4 blocks long","3 blocks long","2 blocks long"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each end must sit on a post.","explain":"4 is longer than the gap, so both ends rest on a post. 3 just fits the gap, so it falls in."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"One post is 3 blocks tall. The other is 1 block tall. What happens to the plank?","choices":["It slopes and can slide off","It stays flat","It gets longer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are the posts the same height?","explain":"The posts are not the same, so the plank slopes. Make both posts the same height."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"The gap is {g} blocks wide. What is the shortest plank, in whole blocks, that rests on both posts?","answer":"g+1","hint":"Just as long as the gap falls in. It must be longer.","explain":"A plank of {g} falls in. One more block, {=g+1}, is longer than the gap, so each end can rest on a post.","params":{"g":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A toy car is a bit taller than {n} blocks. How many blocks tall must each post be to let it under?","answer":"n+1","hint":"The posts must be taller than the car.","explain":"{n} blocks is too low, so each post needs {=n+1} blocks.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which tower is the most steady?","choices":["Wide at the bottom, thin at the top","Thin at the bottom, wide at the top","All the blocks pushed to one side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has the big block at the bottom?","explain":"A wide bottom is steady. Thin at the bottom, or pushed to one side, will tip."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one will stay on top of a tower?","choices":["A cube","A ball","An egg"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has a flat face to sit on?","explain":"A cube has flat faces, so it sits still. A ball and an egg are round, so they roll off."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Your tower is {n} blocks tall. You put 1 more block on top. How tall is it now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count on one more from {n}.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}, so the tower is {=n+1} blocks tall.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You have a big block and a small block. Which goes at the bottom?","choices":["The big block","The small block","It does not matter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about big on small. Will it tip?","explain":"The big block goes at the bottom. A wide bottom is steady. Big on small can tip over."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A bridge has 2 posts. Each post is {n} blocks tall. Then 1 long block goes on top. How many blocks is that?","answer":"2*n+1","hint":"Count the blocks in both posts, then add the long one.","explain":"Two posts of {n} make {=2*n}, and the long block on top makes {=2*n+1}.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some shapes stack. A cube has flat sides. A box has flat sides. Put a flat side on a flat side and it stays.","A ball is round all over. Put it on a block and it rolls off. A cone has a point on top. Nothing can sit on a point. So a cone goes last, right at the top.","Put the big block at the bottom and the small one at the top. Big on small will tip. Line each block up on the one below. If it hangs too far over the edge, it falls.","A bridge needs two posts and a long flat block on top. The block must be longer than the gap, so each end sits on a post. Make the posts the same, and taller than your car."],"adult":["This is early geometry and measures. Development Matters asks children of three and four to talk about 2D and 3D shapes using words such as flat, round, straight, sides and corners. They select shapes for a job, like flat surfaces for building and a prism for a roof. They combine shapes to make new ones, such as an arch, and compare objects by size and length. Positional words such as on top, under, between and next to belong here too.","The maths under the play: a face is a flat surface, a curved surface rolls, and a sphere has no flat face at all. A tower stays up while the middle of each block (its centre of mass) is over the block below. A wide base gives more room for that, which is why wide-bottomed towers are steadier. A bridge plank must span the gap and overlap both supports.","Common mistakes: trying to balance a ball or put a cone in the middle; starting with a small block and putting the big one on top; pushing blocks off-centre until the tower tips; choosing a plank that is just as long as the gap, so it falls in; and building pillars of different heights, so the plank slopes.","In the lab your child drags shapes onto a tower to see which ones stay, builds big-to-small towers, slides a block until its middle is over the one below, picks a plank long enough to make a bridge, and adds blocks to the pillars until a car and a bus can drive under."]},"familyTip":"Build with real blocks, boxes or tins. Before each block goes on, ask: will it stay? Then make a bridge for a toy car and ask how many blocks the posts need.","keyIdea":"Shapes with flat faces (cubes, cuboids, cylinders standing up) stack; a ball (sphere) rolls off and a cone has a point, so it can only go on top. A tower is steadier with the biggest, widest block at the bottom and each block lined up over the one below; a block tips off when its middle goes past the edge. A bridge needs two pillars the same height and a plank longer than the gap, so each end rests on a pillar; to let a toy car under, the pillars must be taller than the car.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Good at the bottom of a tower? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🧱 Flat faces: good at the bottom","⚽ Round or pointy: not at the bottom"],"items":[["big box",0],["ball",1],["brick",0],["orange",1],["cone",1],["thick book",0],["egg",1],["tin of beans, standing up",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtower","caption":"Flagship lab: stack shapes and see which stay, build big-to-small towers, line blocks up, pick a plank for a bridge, and make the pillars tall enough for a car.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OkN4jGLscW":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A triangle is turned upside down. What is it now?","choices":["Still a triangle","A square","A new shape"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count its sides and corners again.","explain":"It still has 3 straight sides and 3 corners. Turning it round does not change it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A shape has 2 long sides and 2 short sides. What is it?","choices":["A rectangle","A square","A triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are all 4 sides the same?","explain":"The sides are not all the same, so it is not a square. 2 long and 2 short is a rectangle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many corners does a circle have?","choices":["None","1","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Run your finger round it. Is there a pointy bit?","explain":"A circle is round all the way, so it has no corners and no straight sides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A square stands on one corner. What is it?","choices":["Still a square","A triangle","Not a shape now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are its 4 sides still all the same?","explain":"Its 4 sides are all still the same, so it is a square, just turned."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A shape has 3 straight sides, but there is a gap at the top. Is it a triangle?","choices":["No, the sides must join up","Yes, it has 3 sides","Yes, it has a corner"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the sides go all the way round?","explain":"A triangle is closed. Its 3 sides must join up with no gap."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which one is a rectangle?","choices":["A door","A plate","Half a sandwich cut corner to corner"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for 4 sides: 2 long and 2 short.","explain":"A door has 2 long sides and 2 short sides. A plate is a circle, and the sandwich half is a triangle."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You make {n} triangles out of sticks. Each one needs 3 sticks. How many sticks do you need?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Count 3 sticks for each triangle.","explain":"{n} triangles need 3 sticks each, so {=3*n} sticks.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which one is a triangle?","choices":["A slice of pizza with straight sides","A wheel","A window"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for 3 straight sides and 3 corners.","explain":"Cut with straight sides, a pizza slice has 3 sides and 3 corners. A wheel is a circle and a window has 4 sides."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"You draw {n} rectangles. Each one has 4 corners. How many corners is that?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Count 4 corners for each rectangle: 4, 8, 12 and so on.","explain":"{n} rectangles with 4 corners each make {=4*n} corners.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many corners does a square have?","choices":["4","3","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Touch each pointy corner and count.","explain":"A square has 4 corners and 4 straight sides, all the same."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You have {n} triangles. How many corners is that in all? Each triangle has 3.","answer":"3*n","hint":"Count 3 for each triangle: 3, 6, 9 and so on.","explain":"{n} triangles with 3 corners each make {=3*n} corners.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which one is a circle?","choices":["A coin","An egg","A book"],"answer":0,"hint":"A circle is round the same all the way.","explain":"A coin is a circle. An egg is round but long, so it is an oval. A book has corners."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You have {n} squares. How many sides is that in all? Each square has 4.","answer":"4*n","hint":"Count 4 for each square: 4, 8 and so on.","explain":"{n} squares with 4 sides each make {=4*n} sides.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Run your finger round a shape. A straight side is like a ruler. A corner is a pointy bit where two sides meet.","A triangle has 3 sides and 3 corners. Tip it over, or make it tall and thin. It is still a triangle. But the sides must all join up and be straight.","A square has 4 sides, all the same. Stand it on a corner and it is still a square. A rectangle has 4 sides too: 2 long and 2 short. A door is a rectangle.","A circle is round all the way, like a plate or a clock. It has no corners. An egg is round too, but it is long. That shape is an oval."],"adult":["This is early shape and space. Development Matters asks three and four year olds to talk about 2D shapes such as circles, rectangles and triangles. They use words like sides, corners, straight, flat and round. In Reception they turn and move shapes. In Year 1 they name common 2D shapes in different sizes and turned different ways.","What matters is the rule, not the picture. A triangle is any closed shape with 3 straight sides. A square has 4 equal sides and 4 square corners (right angles). A rectangle has 4 square corners, and its opposite sides are equal. Strictly, a square is a special rectangle, but at this age children tell them apart by side length. A circle has one curved edge and no corners (vertices).","Common mistakes: calling a square on its point a diamond, as if it were a new shape; thinking only an upright triangle with equal sides counts; calling every 4-sided shape a square; calling an oval a circle; and accepting a shape with a gap or a curved side as a triangle. These slips come from only seeing textbook pictures. Show shapes turned, stretched and in real things.","In the lab your child taps each corner of a triangle, a square and a rectangle to count it, and finds that a circle has none. Then they sort triangles from look-alikes, and squares from rectangles, using marks that show equal sides. They build each shape from short and long sticks, and hunt for the four shapes in a room."]},"familyTip":"Go on a shape walk at home or outside. Point and ask: is that a circle, a square, a triangle or a rectangle? How many corners can you count? Cut a sandwich into triangles, then into squares.","keyIdea":"A triangle has 3 straight sides and 3 corners. A square has 4 straight sides that are all the same length and 4 corners. A rectangle has 4 sides too, 2 long and 2 short. A circle is round all the way, with no straight sides and no corners. Turning a shape round or making it bigger does not change its name, but a gap or a bendy side does. Shapes are all around: a plate is a circle, a window can be a square, a door is a rectangle and half a sandwich can be a triangle.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How many straight sides? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["▲ 3 sides","■ 4 sides","● No sides, round"],"items":[["half a sandwich",0],["a plate",2],["a door",1],["a window",1],["a warning sign",0],["a coin",2],["a book",1],["a wheel",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagshape","caption":"Flagship lab: tap corners to count them, sort real triangles and squares from look-alikes, make shapes from sticks, and hunt for shapes in a room.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Qcp2d_kuta":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which solid has 6 faces that are all the same square?","choices":["A cube","A cuboid","A pyramid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a dice. Are all its faces the same?","explain":"A cube has 6 square faces, all the same. A cuboid has some long rectangle faces."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A tin of beans is like which solid?","choices":["A cylinder","A cuboid","A sphere"],"answer":0,"hint":"A tin has a flat circle at each end and a curved side.","explain":"2 flat circle faces and a curved surface make a cylinder."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam calls a dice a square. What is it really?","choices":["A cube","A circle","A cone"],"answer":0,"hint":"A square is flat. A dice is a solid you can hold.","explain":"A dice is a cube. Each face of a cube is a square, but the whole solid is a cube."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How many flat faces does a sphere have?","choices":["None","1","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is any part of a ball flat?","explain":"A sphere is curved all over, so it has no flat face. That is why it rolls every way."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You press a pyramid's side in paint. What shape is the print?","choices":["A triangle","A circle","No print"],"answer":0,"hint":"The sides of a pyramid are flat and meet at a point.","explain":"A pyramid's side faces are flat triangles, so the print is a triangle."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You have {n} cones. Each cone has 1 flat face. How many flat faces in all?","answer":"n","hint":"Each cone has just 1 flat circle at the bottom. Count the cones.","explain":"1 flat face on each of {n} cones is {n} flat faces.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A cereal box is long. Which solid is it like?","choices":["A cuboid","A cube","A cylinder"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are all its faces the same square, or are some long?","explain":"Its faces are rectangles and not all the same, so it is a cuboid, not a cube."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which of these is a 3-D shape?","choices":["A cone","A triangle","A circle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one can you pick up and hold?","explain":"A cone is a solid. A triangle and a circle are flat 2-D shapes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A box holds {b} balls and {t} tins. How many cylinders are in the box?","answer":"t","hint":"A ball is a sphere. A tin is a cylinder. Count only the tins.","explain":"Only the tins are cylinders, so there are {t}. The {b} balls are spheres.","params":{"b":[1,5],"t":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is a ball called in shape words?","choices":["A sphere","A circle","A cylinder"],"answer":0,"hint":"A circle is flat, like a drawing. A ball is round all over and you can hold it.","explain":"A ball is a sphere. A sphere is a solid that is round all over and has no flat face."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A cube has 6 faces. You put a sticker on {n} faces. How many faces have no sticker?","answer":"6-n","hint":"A cube has 6 faces in all. Take away the {n} with a sticker.","explain":"6 faces take away {n} with a sticker leaves {=6-n} faces with no sticker.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} tins stand on a shelf. Each tin is a cylinder with 2 flat faces. How many flat faces in all?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each tin has a flat circle at the top and one at the bottom. Count in 2s, {n} times.","explain":"{n} tins with 2 flat faces each make {=2*n} flat faces.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which solid has a flat circle face, a curved surface and a point?","choices":["A cone","A pyramid","A cylinder"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a party hat. What shape is its bottom, and what is at the top?","explain":"A cone has 1 flat circle face, 1 curved surface and a point. A pyramid has flat triangle faces, and a cylinder has no point."}],"explain":{"kid":["A solid is a shape you can pick up and hold. A ball, a tin and a box are all solids. A drawing on paper is flat.","Feel a solid. Some parts are flat. We call a flat part a face. Some parts are curved. A curved part can roll.","A cube has 6 flat faces. Each face is a square. You can only see 3 at once, so turn it round to count them all. A pyramid has flat triangle faces that meet at the top.","A ball is a sphere. A tin is a cylinder, with a flat circle at each end. A party hat is a cone. A box can be a cuboid, like a long cube."],"adult":["Naming solids is in US Common Core K.G.A.1 to K.G.A.3 (describe objects using the names of shapes and tell flat, two-dimensional shapes from solid, three-dimensional ones) and in England's Year 1 programme of study (recognise and name common 3-D shapes, including cuboids and cubes, pyramids and spheres). Year 2 then counts faces, edges and vertices.","Use exact words from the start. A face is a flat surface; a cylinder and a cone also have a curved surface, which is not called a face in Year 2 work. A cube is a special cuboid whose 6 faces are all congruent squares. The flat face of a 3-D shape is a 2-D shape, so a cube's face is a square and a cone's base is a circle.","Common mistakes to watch for: calling a solid by a flat name (a ball is a circle, a dice is a square), mixing up a cube and a cuboid, calling a cone a triangle or a pyramid because it looks triangular from the side, counting only the 3 faces you can see on a cube, and thinking a round solid has no flat face at all.","In the lab your child turns a cube and taps each face to count all 6, taps the flat faces and curved surfaces of a cylinder and a cone, names six solids, picks which solid made a paint print, and matches a tin, a ball, a dice, a cereal box and a party hat to their solids."]},"familyTip":"Go on a solid hunt in the kitchen: find a cylinder (a tin), a cuboid (a cereal box) and a sphere (an orange). Press a flat face of each into a little paint or flour and look at the print. Ask: which one did not make a print, and why?","keyIdea":"A 3-D shape is a solid you can hold, not a flat drawing. A cube has 6 flat faces that are all the same square; a cuboid is a stretched cube with some long rectangle faces. A sphere, like a ball, is round all over with no flat face. A cylinder, like a tin, has 2 flat circle faces and a curved surface; a cone has 1 flat circle face, a curved surface and a point. A pyramid has flat triangle faces that meet at a point. You cannot see every face at once, so turn a solid to count them.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it have a curved surface? Drag each solid, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Only flat faces","Has a curved surface"],"items":[["cube",0],["cylinder",1],["cuboid",0],["sphere",1],["pyramid",0],["cone",1],["dice",0],["tin of beans",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsolid","caption":"Flagship lab: turn a cube to count its faces, find flat faces and curved surfaces, name six solids, match paint prints and find solids at home.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_hR2Y7NhMSY":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many lines of symmetry does a square have?","choices":["4","2","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the straight folds and the corner to corner folds.","explain":"4: up and down, across, and both diagonals. All four folds make the halves match."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many lines of symmetry does an equilateral triangle have?","choices":["3","1","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"It has 3 equal sides. Try a fold from each corner.","explain":"3: each fold goes from a corner to the middle of the opposite side."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which capital letter has a horizontal line of symmetry?","choices":["B","A","F"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fold across the middle. Do the top and bottom match?","explain":"B: its top and bottom halves match. A has a vertical line instead, and F has none."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Half a symmetric shape has {n} shaded squares, and none of them touch the mirror line. How many squares does the whole shape have?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each square gets one partner on the other side.","explain":"{n} squares and {n} partners make {=2*n} squares in the whole shape.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A square touches a vertical mirror line on its left side. Where is its reflection?","choices":["Touching the line on the right side","At the right edge of the grid","One square away from the line"],"answer":0,"hint":"How far from the line is the square?","explain":"It is 0 squares from the line, so its partner touches the line on the other side."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A horizontal mirror line runs between rows 4 and 5. A square is in row {r}. Which row is its reflection in?","answer":"9-r","hint":"Count how many rows row {r} is above the line, then go that far below row 5.","explain":"Row {r} is {=4-r} rows above the line, so its reflection is in row {=9-r}.","params":{"r":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A diagonal mirror line goes from the top-left to the bottom-right corner. The square in row 1, column 4 reflects to which square?","choices":["Row 4, column 1","Row 1, column 1","Row 8, column 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"With this diagonal mirror, the row and column numbers swap.","explain":"Row 1, column 4 becomes row 4, column 1. Flipping straight up or down is the slip."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How many lines of symmetry does a slanted parallelogram with no right angles, and long and short sides, have?","choices":["0","2","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try each fold. Do the corners ever meet?","explain":"0: no fold makes the halves match, not even corner to corner."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Tom says mirror lines are always up and down. Is he right?","choices":["No, they can go across or slant too","Yes, always up and down","Only on squared paper"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the letter B and the square folded corner to corner.","explain":"No. B has a horizontal line, and a square has 2 diagonal lines of symmetry."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many lines of symmetry does a rectangle that is not a square have?","choices":["2","4","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fold it corner to corner in your head. Do the corners meet?","explain":"2: across the middle and up and down. The diagonals do not work because the corners do not meet."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A shaded square is {d} squares to the left of a vertical mirror line. How many squares to the right of the line is its reflection?","answer":"d","hint":"Count from the mirror line, not from the edge of the grid.","explain":"A reflection keeps the same distance from the mirror line, so it is {d} squares to the right, in the same row.","params":{"d":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"The mirror line runs between columns 5 and 6 of a grid. A square is in column {c}. Which column is its reflection in?","answer":"11-c","hint":"Find how far column {c} is from the line, then go the same distance past column 6.","explain":"Column {c} is {=6-c} columns left of the line, so its reflection is in column {=11-c}.","params":{"c":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia copies the left half onto the right half without flipping it. What has she done?","choices":["She slid it, so it is not a reflection","She made a correct reflection","She rotated it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a square next to the mirror line stay next to it?","explain":"Sliding moves every square the same way. A mirror flips the half, so squares near the line stay near the line."}],"explain":{"kid":["Fold a shape in half. If the two halves match exactly, the fold is a line of symmetry. We also call it a mirror line.","Some shapes have more than one. A square has 4. A rectangle has only 2. Fold a rectangle corner to corner and the corners do not meet.","To finish a shape on squared paper, count each square from the mirror line. Its partner is the same number of squares away on the other side. A square that touches the line has a partner that touches it too.","Do not slide the half across. A mirror flips it. And the mirror line can lie flat or slant, not just stand up."],"adult":["In England, Year 4 pupils identify lines of symmetry in 2-D shapes presented in different orientations, and complete a simple symmetric figure with respect to a specific line of symmetry. Year 2 meets line symmetry in a vertical line first. In the US, Common Core 4.G.A.3 defines a line of symmetry as a line across the figure such that the figure can be folded along the line into matching parts.","The key fact is distance. A reflection keeps every point the same distance from the mirror line, measured straight across at a right angle to it. On squared paper with a vertical line, a square 2 columns to the left lands 2 columns to the right, in the same row. With a diagonal line from corner to corner, the row and column numbers swap.","Common mistakes to watch for: saying a rectangle has 4 lines because the diagonals look like they cut it in half; sliding the given half across instead of flipping it; counting from the edge of the grid instead of from the mirror line; and reflecting straight across when the mirror line slants. Folding paper or using a small mirror checks each one.","In the lab you fold a rectangle on four lines and see which two work, then shade squares to complete figures across a vertical, a horizontal and a diagonal mirror line. Wrong squares turn red and the feedback names the slip."]},"familyTip":"Fold a sheet of paper in half, cut out a shape along the fold and open it: the fold is a line of symmetry. Then hunt at home for shapes and letters with 0, 1, 2 or more lines.","keyIdea":"A line of symmetry is a fold line: fold the shape on it and one half lands exactly on the other. On squared paper, a mirror line reflects each square to a partner the same distance from the line, on the other side, so a square next to the line stays next to the line. A rectangle has 2 lines of symmetry, not 4, because folding on a diagonal does not make the corners meet. Mirror lines can be vertical, horizontal or diagonal.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the dashed line a line of symmetry? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Line of symmetry","Not a line of symmetry"],"items":[["Square, corner to corner",0],["Rectangle, corner to corner",1],["Rectangle, across the middle",0],["Letter A, up and down",0],["Letter A, across the middle",1],["Letter F, up and down",1],["Equilateral triangle, corner to the middle of a side",0],["Slanted parallelogram, corner to corner",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsymm","caption":"Flagship lab: fold a rectangle to find its lines of symmetry, then complete figures on squared paper across vertical, horizontal and diagonal mirror lines.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_e4V6hvcuEJ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Three angles meet at a point. Two of them are {a}° and {b}°. What is the third angle?","answer":"360-a-b","hint":"Angles round a point make a full turn, 360°. Take away both angles you know.","explain":"360 − {a} − {b} = {=360-a-b}, so the third angle is {=360-a-b}°.","params":{"a":[100,150],"b":[60,110]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A clock hand turns through {n} right angles. How many degrees is that?","answer":"n*90","hint":"Each right angle is a quarter turn, which is 90°.","explain":"{n} × 90 = {=n*90}, so the hand turns {=n*90}°. Four right angles would be a full turn of 360°.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Angle P is 50° with arms 2 cm long. Angle Q is 50° with arms 8 cm long. Which is bigger?","choices":["Q, its arms are longer","P, its arms are shorter","They are the same size"],"answer":2,"hint":"An angle measures the turn between the arms, not how long they are.","explain":"Both turn through 50°, so they are the same size. Longer arms do not make a bigger angle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In triangle ABC, which angle is ∠BCA?","choices":["The angle at B","The angle at C","The angle at A"],"answer":1,"hint":"With three letters, the vertex of the angle is always the middle letter.","explain":"The middle letter of ∠BCA is C, so it is the angle at C, between sides CB and CA."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A right angle is drawn tilted, so neither arm is flat. What type of angle is it?","choices":["Still a right angle","Acute","Obtuse"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turning the whole angle round does not change how far one arm is from the other.","explain":"It is still 90°, a right angle. The direction an angle faces does not change its type."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ravi says two angles on a straight line are 100° and 260°. What went wrong?","choices":["He used 360° instead of 180°","Nothing, that is right","He should add 100 and 180"],"answer":0,"hint":"A straight line is a half turn. How many degrees is a half turn?","explain":"Angles on a straight line add up to 180°, so the other angle is 80°. He used the full turn, 360°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Two angles in a triangle have the same arc mark. One is 40°. What do you know about the other?","choices":["It is also 40°","It is 140°","It is 80°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Matching arcs tell you which angles are equal, just as ticks do for sides.","explain":"Matching arcs mean equal angles, so the other one is also 40°. No measuring is needed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which of these is a reflex angle?","choices":["210°","175°","90°"],"answer":0,"hint":"A reflex angle is more than a straight line, which is 180°.","explain":"210° is more than 180°, so it is reflex. 175° is obtuse and 90° is a right angle."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Two angles sit on a straight line. One is {a}°. How many degrees is the other angle, x?","answer":"180-a","hint":"Angles on a straight line add up to 180°. Take {a} away from 180.","explain":"180 − {a} = {=180-a}, so x = {=180-a}°. Check: {a} + {=180-a} = 180.","params":{"a":[25,155]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A right angle, marked with a small square, is split into two parts. One part is {a}°. How big is the other part?","answer":"90-a","hint":"The small square means the whole corner is 90°. Subtract the part you know.","explain":"90 − {a} = {=90-a}, so the other part is {=90-a}°. Both parts together make the right angle.","params":{"a":[15,75]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"An angle is 135°. What type of angle is it?","choices":["Acute","Obtuse","Reflex"],"answer":1,"hint":"Is it more than a right angle (90°)? Is it less than a straight line (180°)?","explain":"135° is more than 90° and less than 180°, so it is obtuse. Acute is under 90° and reflex is over 180°."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"In a diagram, sides PQ and RS each have two tick marks. What does that tell you?","choices":["PQ and RS are each 2 cm","PQ and RS are the same length","PQ and RS are parallel"],"answer":1,"hint":"Ticks do not give a length. They pair up sides with the same number of marks.","explain":"Matching tick marks mean equal lengths, so PQ = RS. Parallel lines are shown with arrows, not ticks."}],"explain":{"kid":["Stand up and face the door. Turn until you face the side wall. That quarter turn is a right angle. It is 90°. On a drawing it gets a small square in the corner. Two right angles make a half turn, a straight line of 180°. Four make a full turn of 360°.","Angles have names. Smaller than a right angle is acute. Between a right angle and a straight line is obtuse. More than a straight line is reflex. Turning the page does not change the type. A tilted right angle is still a right angle.","The size of an angle is the turn, not the length of the arms. If you draw longer arms on the same angle, the opening stays the same. Do not let long arms fool you.","Maths drawings use marks. Sides with the same number of ticks are the same length. Angles with the same arc are equal. In ∠ABC the angle is at B, the middle letter. Use the marks to find missing angles: on a straight line the angles add up to 180°."],"adult":["This lesson follows the English national curriculum for Years 3 to 6. In Year 3 pupils 'recognise angles as a property of shape or a description of a turn', 'identify right angles, recognise that two right angles make a half-turn, three make three quarters of a turn and four a complete turn', and 'identify whether angles are greater than or less than a right angle'. In Year 4 they 'identify acute and obtuse angles and compare and order angles up to two right angles by size'. In Year 6 they find missing angles. US Common Core 4.MD.5 to 4.MD.7 treat angle measure as turn in degrees and as additive.","The diagram conventions are standard. A small square marks a right angle. Single or double ticks show sides of equal length. Matching arcs show equal angles. An angle is named with three letters such as ∠ABC (the vertex is always the middle letter) or with a single letter or number. Facts that come from these marks, such as angles on a straight line adding to 180° and angles round a point adding to 360°, let pupils find unknown angles without a protractor.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking that longer arms or a bigger drawn arc make a bigger angle; only recognising a right angle when one arm is horizontal; reading a tick as 1 cm or as a parallel mark (parallel lines use arrows); naming ∠ABC as the angle at A; and using 360° for angles on a straight line. Ask 'how far has it turned?' rather than 'how big does it look?', and turn the page to show that orientation does not matter.","In the lab you turn an arm to make one, two and three right angles, sort tilted and reflex angles by type, compare angles with arms of different lengths, read the marks on an isosceles triangle, and use the marks to find a missing angle on a straight line, inside a right angle and round a point."]},"familyTip":"Make a right-angle checker by folding a scrap of paper in half and then in half again. Hunt round the house together for corners that are right angles, smaller (acute) or bigger (obtuse). Then open a door or a laptop slowly and call out each type as the angle grows.","keyIdea":"An angle measures turn: how far one arm has turned from the other around the vertex. A quarter turn is a right angle (90°), marked with a small square. Acute angles are smaller than 90°, obtuse angles are between 90° and 180°, a straight line is 180° and reflex angles are more than 180°. The length of the arms does not change the angle. Diagrams use marks so you can read facts without measuring: matching ticks mean equal sides, matching arcs mean equal angles, and in ∠ABC the middle letter, B, is the vertex.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What type of angle is it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Acute","Right","Obtuse","Reflex"],"items":[["30°",0],["The corner of a book",1],["A quarter turn",1],["120°",2],["89°",0],["170°",2],["270°",3],["Three quarters of a turn",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagangle","caption":"Flagship lab: turn an arm, sort angles by type, test longer arms, read diagram marks and find missing angles.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_SH7QgFl8-v":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"After a translation, how does the new shape compare with the old one?","choices":["same size and shape","bigger","a different shape"],"answer":0,"hint":"A translation only slides. Does sliding stretch anything?","explain":"Translations, rotations and reflections keep lengths and angles, so the image is congruent: same size and shape."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which move makes a mirror image that faces the other way?","choices":["a reflection","a translation","a rotation"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what you see in a mirror.","explain":"A reflection flips the shape in a mirror line, so the image faces the other way."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Corner A is at ({a}, 1). The shape is translated {b} squares right. What is the first number of A's new position?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Moving right adds to the first number.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so A moves to ({=a+b}, 1).","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How many quarter turns make a half turn?","choices":["2","4","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"A quarter turn is 90°. A half turn is 180°.","explain":"Two 90° turns make 180°, which is a half turn."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A shape makes a half turn about a point. Does it matter if you turn clockwise or anticlockwise?","choices":["no, it ends the same","yes, it ends in a different place","only if the shape is a square"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try 180° one way and 180° the other way on a clock face.","explain":"Half a turn either way reaches the same place, because 180° is exactly half of a full turn."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A point moves from (1, {=a+b}) to (1, {a}). How many squares down did it move?","answer":"b","hint":"Only the second number changed. Find the difference.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b}, so it moved {b} squares down.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"To describe a rotation, which facts do you need?","choices":["centre, angle and direction","only the angle","the mirror line"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does it turn about, how far, and which way?","explain":"A rotation needs its centre, its angle (such as 90°) and its direction (clockwise or anticlockwise)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A pupil says the shape moved 2 squares because there are 2 empty squares between it and its image. The shape is 3 squares wide. What should they do?","choices":["count how far one corner moves","count only the gap","count the squares inside the shape"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick one corner and follow it to the same corner of the image.","explain":"Count the distance a corner moves, not the gap. Here it is 2 + 3 = 5 squares."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A shape slides 3 squares to the right without turning. What is this move called?","choices":["a translation","a rotation","a reflection"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the shape turn or flip, or did it only change place?","explain":"Sliding without turning or flipping is a translation. Every square moved 3 to the right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Point (2, {a}) is translated 5 squares right and {b} squares up. What is the second number of its new position?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Moving up adds to the second number. Moving right changes only the first.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so the point lands on (7, {=a+b}).","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A shape is translated {a} squares left and then {b} more squares left. How many squares left has it moved in total?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Both moves go the same way, so join them together.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} squares to the left. Two translations make one translation.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A flagpole points up. The flag makes a quarter turn clockwise. Which way does the pole point now?","choices":["right","left","down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a clock hand at 12 moving a quarter turn clockwise.","explain":"A quarter turn clockwise takes 12 o'clock to 3 o'clock, so the pole points to the right."}],"explain":{"kid":["A shape can move on squared paper in three ways. It can slide. It can turn. It can flip.","A slide is called a translation. Every square moves the same way. Count how far one corner goes: some squares across, then some up or down.","A turn is called a rotation. The shape spins round a dot that stays still. A quarter turn is like a clock hand going from 12 to 3. Two quarter turns make a half turn.","A flip is called a reflection. The shape lands like a mirror picture and faces the other way. Be careful: after all three moves the shape is just as big. Only its place, or the way it faces, changes."],"adult":["In England's maths curriculum, Year 4 pupils 'describe movements between positions as translations of a given unit to the left/right and up/down'. In Year 6 they 'draw and translate simple shapes on the coordinate plane, and reflect them in the axes'. Key Stage 3 adds rotations about a centre and asks pupils to identify congruent shapes made by rotations, reflections and translations.","A translation is fixed by one movement: how far across and how far up or down. Every point of the shape moves by that amount, so one arrow from a corner to its image says it all. Later this becomes a column vector. A rotation needs three facts: the centre, the angle, and the direction. A reflection needs a mirror line. Each of these moves keeps side lengths and angles, so the image is congruent.","Common mistakes: children often count the gap between the shape and its image instead of the distance a corner moves. Many swap across and up, so (2, 3) moved 4 right and 1 up becomes (3, 7). With rotations they forget the centre, or mix up clockwise and anticlockwise. Some also call any mirror image a rotation. Asking 'which way does it face now?' helps here.","In the lab your child slides a flag onto an outline, 4 right and 2 up. Then they draw the arrow from corner A to its image to describe a slide. Next they give the flag a quarter turn and a half turn about a dot. Last, they pick which copy is a reflection, a rotation or a translation."]},"familyTip":"Draw a 6 by 6 grid and cut a small L shape from card. Take turns calling out moves, such as '3 right, 1 up' or 'quarter turn clockwise about this corner', while the other moves the card. Trace each copy and ask: is it the same size? Which way is it facing?","keyIdea":"A transformation moves a shape to a new position on a grid. A translation slides every point the same distance in the same direction, described as squares left or right and up or down (or by a vector). A rotation turns the shape about a fixed centre of rotation by an angle such as a quarter turn (90°) or a half turn (180°), clockwise or anticlockwise. A reflection flips it in a mirror line into a mirror image. All three keep lengths and angles, so the image is congruent to the original.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Translation, rotation or reflection? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Translation","Rotation","Reflection"],"items":[["A chess rook moving 3 squares forward",0],["A lift going up 2 floors",0],["A drawer sliding out",0],["A clock hand moving from 12 to 3",1],["A wheel turning a half turn",1],["A door swinging open on its hinge",1],["Your face in a mirror",2],["Butterfly wings folded onto each other",2],["A left glove compared with a right glove",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagslide","caption":"Flagship lab: slide a flag onto an outline, draw the arrow that describes a slide, turn it a quarter and a half turn, then name the move.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_vnJEztczji":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many distinct nets does a cube have?","choices":["11","6","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"There are more than you might first think.","explain":"There are 11 distinct nets of a cube (counting rotations and reflections as the same)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A 2-by-3 rectangle of squares…","choices":["is not a cube net","is a cube net","is a cuboid net"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fold it in your head: faces crash.","explain":"Folding a 2-by-3 block makes faces overlap, so it is not a cube net."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the net of a cylinder (roughly)?","choices":["a rectangle with a circle at each end","6 squares","2 triangles and 3 rectangles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Unwrap a kitchen-roll tube.","explain":"The curved surface unwraps to a rectangle; the two ends are circles."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Tabs on a cardboard net are for…","choices":["gluing faces together","counting as extra faces","making the solid heavier"],"answer":0,"hint":"They are flaps, not faces.","explain":"Tabs are for sticking. They are not faces of the solid, so do not count them in the net of faces."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A triangular prism has…","choices":["2 triangular faces and 3 rectangular faces","6 square faces","1 triangular face only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a tent shape. Count faces and check whether folding would overlap.","explain":"Two ends are triangles; three sides are rectangles (or parallelograms)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A net shows {a} faces of a solid that has {b} faces in total. How many faces are missing from the net?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Subtract. Count faces and check whether folding would overlap.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} faces are missing, so it is not a complete net.","params":{"a":[3,5],"b":[6,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Lee draws 7 squares joined edge to edge and says it is a cube net. Is Lee right?","choices":["No, a cube has only 6 faces","Yes, extras fold inside","Yes, if one is a tab"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the faces of a cube.","explain":"A cube has exactly 6 faces, so a net of 7 squares is not a cube net. A tab is not a face."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"On a cuboid net, two faces that share an edge on the solid must…","choices":["share an edge in the net, or be joinable by folding","be the same colour","be squares"],"answer":0,"hint":"Folding brings edges together.","explain":"Faces that meet on the solid meet along an edge in the net or after a fold. Matching edge lengths matter on a cuboid."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"One net has {a} faces and another has {b}. How many faces across both nets?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the faces on the first net to the faces on the second.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} faces drawn in total.","params":{"a":[4,6],"b":[3,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many faces does a cube have?","choices":["6","4","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the sides of a dice.","explain":"A cube has 6 square faces: front, back, left, right, top and bottom."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A net is…","choices":["a flat pattern that folds into a 3-D shape","a drawing of a solid from an angle","a list of edge lengths"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of an opened cardboard box.","explain":"A net is the flat 2-D pattern of faces that folds into the solid."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which cannot be a cube net?","choices":["a straight row of 6 squares","a cross of 6 squares","a zigzag of 6 squares"],"answer":0,"hint":"When you fold a long row, the ends meet in a problem.","explain":"A straight row of 6 folds into a tube with two faces left over that have nowhere to go. Crosses and many zigzags do work."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A prism has {a} rectangular side faces and {b} end faces. How many faces in total?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the rectangular side faces to the two ends (or however many ends).","explain":"{a} rectangular faces + {b} ends = {=a+b} faces on the net and on the solid.","params":{"a":[3,5],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p6","prompt":"A drawing shows {a} squares joined, which is {b} more than a cube needs. How many squares are on the drawing?","answer":"a","hint":"Start from a real cube net (6 squares) and add the extras you drew by mistake.","explain":"The drawing has {a} squares. A cube needs only 6, so this has {b} too many and is not a cube net.","params":{"a":[5,7],"b":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A net is a flat drawing that folds up into a solid. Think of an open cardboard box flattened out.","A cube has 6 square faces. Its net is 6 squares joined edge to edge. When you fold them, they make the cube.","Not every pattern of 6 squares works. If two squares would land on the same face, or if a square sticks out where there is no room, it is not a net.","A cuboid is a stretched cube. Its net uses rectangles that match the front, side and top faces. Match the sizes before you fold."],"adult":["In the National Curriculum for England, Year 5 Geometry asks pupils to identify 3-D shapes, including cubes and other cuboids, from 2-D representations. Nets appear in Year 6 and KS3 as a standard way to link surface area to the faces of a prism. This lesson is about recognising and testing nets, not yet about calculating surface area.","A valid net places every face of the solid once, joined along edges that really meet on the solid, so that folding does not make two faces occupy the same place. There are 11 distinct nets of a cube. Crosses, zigzags and branched chains appear; a straight row of 6 squares never works, and a 2-by-3 rectangle of squares never works either.","Common mistakes: thinking any 6 squares make a cube net; forgetting to check that faces have matching edge lengths on a cuboid; counting tabs or flaps as faces; and confusing a net with an isometric drawing of the solid.","In the lab your child folds a cube net, rejects two false cube nets, builds a cuboid net from matching rectangles, and matches three nets to the solids they make."]},"familyTip":"Open a clean cereal box carefully along the glued edges and flatten it. Count the faces and see how they join. Then try to sketch the net before you fold it back. Kitchen roll tubes are cylinders: their net is a rectangle plus two circles.","keyIdea":"A net is a flat 2-D pattern that folds into a 3-D shape. The faces of the solid are drawn joined edge to edge so that when you fold along the edges, every face meets without overlapping. A cube net has 6 equal squares; only some arrangements of 6 squares fold into a cube. A cuboid net has 6 rectangles that match its faces. Checking that faces match, and that they do not pile on top of each other when folded, is how you test a net.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Cube net or not? Drag each pattern, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Folds into a cube","Does not fold into a cube"],"items":[["A cross of 6 squares",0],["A zigzag of 6 squares",0],["A straight row of 6 squares",1],["A 2-by-3 block of squares",1],["A T of 6 squares",0],["Four squares in a row with one on each side of the second",0],["Seven squares joined",1],["Five squares joined",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnets","caption":"Flagship lab: fold a cube net, reject two false nets, build a cuboid net from matching rectangles, and match three nets to their solids.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Y9XKzLrUAZ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many quarter turns make a half turn?","choices":["2","1","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Half the circle is two of the four equal parts.","explain":"Two quarter turns make a half turn. Four quarter turns make a whole turn."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You face the door and make a half turn. Where do you face now?","choices":["behind you, away from the door","the door","to your side"],"answer":0,"hint":"A half turn goes halfway round the circle.","explain":"A half turn leaves you facing the opposite way, so the door is behind you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Pip faces the tree. Which turn makes Pip face the tree again?","choices":["a whole turn","a half turn","a three-quarter turn"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which turn goes all the way round the circle?","explain":"Only a whole turn, four quarter turns, brings Pip back to the tree. A half turn faces the pond."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A three-quarter turn clockwise ends in the same place as…","choices":["a quarter turn anticlockwise","a quarter turn clockwise","a half turn"],"answer":0,"hint":"Three quarters one way leaves one quarter left to go round.","explain":"Three quarter turns clockwise and one quarter turn anticlockwise both end at the same spot, the ball, if you start at the tree."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia makes a half turn clockwise. Sam makes a half turn anticlockwise. Both start facing the board. What do they face at the end?","choices":["the same way: away from the board","different ways","the board"],"answer":0,"hint":"Halfway round a circle is the same spot from either side.","explain":"A half turn ends facing behind you whichever way you turn, so both face away from the board."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Anticlockwise means turning…","choices":["the other way from clock hands","the same way as clock hands","a whole turn"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Anti' means against, so it goes against the clock hands.","explain":"Anticlockwise is the opposite way to the hands on a clock."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A clock hand points at 12. It makes {n} quarter turns clockwise. How many quarter turns is that short of {k} whole turns?","answer":"4*k-n","hint":"Each whole turn is 4 quarter turns. Work out how many quarter turns {k} whole turns need first.","explain":"{k} whole turns are {=4*k} quarter turns, and {=4*k} − {n} = {=4*k-n}.","params":{"n":[1,3],"k":[1,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Pip faces the tree and turns a quarter turn clockwise. Leo says 'Pip has now made a half turn because Pip faces a new thing.' Is Leo right?","choices":["No, it is one quarter turn","Yes, it is a half turn","Yes, it is a whole turn"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the quarter turns, not the things Pip faces.","explain":"Pip turned one quarter of the circle. A half turn needs two quarter turns and leaves Pip facing behind."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Pip makes {n} whole turns and then one more half turn. How many half turns is that altogether?","answer":"2*n+1","hint":"Each whole turn is 2 half turns. Count those first, then add the extra half turn.","explain":"{n} whole turns are {=2*n} half turns, and one more makes {=2*n+1} half turns.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You face the window and make a whole turn. What do you face now?","choices":["the window","the wall behind you","the wall at your side"],"answer":0,"hint":"A whole turn goes all the way round the circle.","explain":"A whole turn brings you back to face the way you started, so you face the window again."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which way is clockwise?","choices":["the way the hands on a clock go","the other way from clock hands","straight forwards"],"answer":0,"hint":"The word has 'clock' in it. Think of the clock hands moving.","explain":"Clockwise means turning the same way as the hands on a clock: from 12 round to 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"How many quarter turns are there in {n} whole turns?","answer":"4*n","hint":"One whole turn is 4 quarter turns. Count 4 for each whole turn.","explain":"Each whole turn has 4 quarter turns, so {n} whole turns have {=4*n} quarter turns.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"How many half turns make {n} whole turns?","answer":"2*n","hint":"One whole turn is 2 half turns. Count 2 for each whole turn.","explain":"Each whole turn has 2 half turns, so {n} whole turns have {=2*n} half turns.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Pip faces the tree. The house is a quarter turn clockwise. Pip turns a quarter turn anticlockwise. Does Pip face the house?","choices":["No, Pip turned the other way","Yes, any quarter turn works","Yes, but only if Pip turns fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clockwise and anticlockwise go opposite ways.","explain":"The house is clockwise from the tree. An anticlockwise quarter turn goes the other way, so Pip faces the ball instead."}],"explain":{"kid":["Stand up and face the door. Now turn all the way round until you face the door again. That is a whole turn.","Cut the whole turn into four equal bits. One bit is a quarter turn. Two bits make a half turn. Now you face behind you.","Three bits make a three-quarter turn. Four bits make a whole turn, and you are back where you began.","Clockwise means you turn the same way as the hands on a clock. Anticlockwise means you turn the other way."],"adult":["Year 1 pupils in England learn to 'describe position, direction and movement, including whole, half, quarter and three-quarter turns'. Year 2 adds the words clockwise and anticlockwise and links a quarter turn to a right angle. Words to use: turn, whole turn, half turn, quarter turn, three-quarter turn, clockwise, anticlockwise, left, right, forwards, backwards, face.","Common mistakes: thinking a whole turn leaves you facing backwards (that is a half turn); counting the starting position as the first quarter; mixing up clockwise and anticlockwise; and not seeing that three quarter turns clockwise end in the same place as one quarter turn anticlockwise. A half turn ends in the same place whichever way you go.","In the lab your child turns Pip one quarter at a time, by button or by dragging round Pip. A shaded wedge fills a quarter, a half, three quarters or the whole circle, so the size of the turn is something they can see. Then they turn Pip to face the house, the ball and the pond, choosing the shortest turn each time."]},"familyTip":"Play 'Robot': your child is the robot and you give orders such as 'quarter turn clockwise' or 'half turn'. Put a teddy, a cup, a book and a shoe on four sides of them, and ask 'What are you facing now?' after each turn. Then swap, and let them give the orders.","keyIdea":"A turn is measured by how much of a whole circle you go round. A quarter turn is one of four equal parts, a half turn is two quarter turns and leaves you facing behind you, a three-quarter turn is three quarter turns, and a whole turn is four quarter turns that bring you back to face the way you started. Clockwise means turning the same way as the hands on a clock; anticlockwise is the other way.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"After this turn, do you face the same way as at the start? Drag each turn, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Same way as the start","A new way"],"items":[["a whole turn",0],["a half turn",1],["a quarter turn",1],["four quarter turns",0],["a three-quarter turn",1],["two whole turns",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagturn","caption":"Flagship lab: turn Pip by quarter turns, watch the wedge fill, try anticlockwise, then face what you are asked.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_u23IGDxOpk":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The letter T has…","choices":["perpendicular lines only","parallel lines only","no straight lines"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where the top bar meets the stem, is the corner square?","explain":"The top of T meets the stem in square corners, so the lines are perpendicular. T has no pair of lines going the same way."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many pairs of parallel sides does a rectangle have?","choices":["2","1","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the top and bottom, then the two sides.","explain":"The top and bottom are parallel, and the left and right sides are parallel. That is 2 pairs."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"On a dot grid, line A goes {a} dots across and 1 dot up. A perpendicular line goes 1 dot across. How many dots down does it go?","answer":"a","hint":"A perpendicular line turns the steps round: the across and up numbers swap.","explain":"Turning A's steps gives 1 across and {a} down, so the new line meets A in a square corner.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A flagpole stands straight up from flat ground. The pole is…","choices":["vertical","horizontal","parallel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it flat, or straight up and down?","explain":"Straight up and down is vertical. Parallel describes a pair of lines, not one pole."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A short line and a long line both lie flat, one above the other. Are they parallel?","choices":["Yes, they go the same way","No, they are not the same length","No, they are perpendicular"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does length change which way a line goes?","explain":"Both go the same way, so the gap stays the same. Length does not matter for parallel lines."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which letter has a pair of parallel lines but no right angle?","choices":["N","L","V"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for two strokes going the same way, with no square corner.","explain":"N has two parallel uprights, and its slant meets them at sharp corners, not right angles. L has a right angle; V has no parallel lines."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A picture of a fence post is turned on its side. In the turned picture, the post is…","choices":["horizontal","still vertical","perpendicular"],"answer":0,"hint":"Horizontal and vertical compare a line with the ground at the bottom of the page.","explain":"Turned on its side, the post now lies flat on the page, so it is horizontal. The names depend on which way the line points."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"The rungs of a ladder go across between its two long sides. Each rung is…","choices":["perpendicular to the sides and parallel to the other rungs","parallel to the sides","neither parallel nor perpendicular to anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the corner where a rung meets a side, then compare two rungs.","explain":"A rung meets each side in a square corner, so it is perpendicular to the sides. All the rungs go the same way, so they are parallel to each other."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"On a dot grid, line A goes 1 dot across and {a} dots up. A parallel line goes 2 dots across. How many dots up does it go?","answer":"2*a","hint":"A parallel line keeps the same steps. Two lots of 1 across need two lots of the up steps.","explain":"Each 1 dot across goes with {a} dots up, so 2 dots across go with {=2*a} dots up. The line goes the same way as A.","params":{"a":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The two rails of a train track never meet and stay the same distance apart. The rails are…","choices":["parallel","perpendicular","vertical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the rails go the same way, or meet in a square corner?","explain":"The rails go the same way and keep the same gap, so they are parallel. Vertical describes one line going straight up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Two parallel lines are {a} cm apart at one end. How many centimetres apart are they at the other end?","answer":"a","hint":"What stays the same all along a pair of parallel lines?","explain":"Parallel lines keep the same gap all along, so they are {a} cm apart at the other end too.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"On a dot grid, line A goes {a} dots across for every 1 dot up. You draw a parallel line. How many dots across does it go for every 1 dot up?","answer":"a","hint":"A parallel line goes the same way, so it copies the steps.","explain":"A parallel line copies the steps of line A: {a} dots across for every 1 dot up.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Line A is slanted. Line B crosses it and makes a square corner. Which is true?","choices":["A and B are perpendicular","They cannot be perpendicular because A is slanted","A and B are parallel"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes two lines perpendicular: their tilt, or the corner they make?","explain":"A square corner is a right angle, so A and B are perpendicular. Perpendicular lines can both be slanted."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Mia says two lines are parallel because they do not touch on the page. But the gap between them gets smaller. Is she right?","choices":["No, if they were longer they would meet","Yes, they do not touch","Yes, if they are the same length"],"answer":0,"hint":"Imagine drawing both lines longer.","explain":"The gap is shrinking, so longer lines would meet. Parallel lines keep the same gap and never meet."}],"explain":{"kid":["A horizontal line lies flat, like the floor or the top of a table. A vertical line goes straight up and down, like a flagpole. Lines that are not flat or straight up are slanted.","Two lines are parallel if they go the same way. The gap between them is the same all along, so they never meet. Train rails are parallel. Parallel lines can be long or short, and flat, upright or slanted.","Two lines are perpendicular if they meet in a square corner. That corner is a right angle. The corner of a book shows it. The lines can both be slanted, as long as the corner is square.","Be careful with lines that do not touch on the page. If the gap gets smaller, they would meet if you drew them longer. So they are not parallel."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. This matches Year 3 Geometry in the National Curriculum in England (identify horizontal and vertical lines and pairs of perpendicular and parallel lines) and CCSS 4.G.1 (draw and identify perpendicular and parallel lines). It builds on right angles as a quarter turn and leads into the properties of 2-D shapes, such as a rectangle having two pairs of parallel sides and four right angles.","Horizontal and vertical describe one line compared with the ground. Parallel and perpendicular describe a pair of lines compared with each other. Parallel lines keep a constant distance apart and never meet. Perpendicular lines meet at a right angle. Neither idea depends on how long the lines are or on which way the page is turned.","Common mistakes: thinking parallel lines must be the same length, or must be flat. Calling two lines parallel because they do not touch on the page, even though the gap is closing. Thinking perpendicular means one flat line and one upright line, so a tilted square corner is missed. And mixing up the two words.","In the lab your child turns a stick until it is flat and then upright. Next they turn line B until its gap to line A is the same at both ends; dashed lines show where tilted lines would meet. They pick the true parallel pairs from look-alikes and turn a line to make a square corner with a slanted line. Then they sort the letters T, Z, H, V and N, and draw a parallel and a perpendicular line on a dot grid."]},"familyTip":"On a walk or at home, hunt for pairs of lines: rails and fence posts for parallel, window corners and table legs on the floor for perpendicular. Ask: do they go the same way, or meet in a square corner?","keyIdea":"A horizontal line lies flat, like the ground, and a vertical line goes straight up and down. Parallel lines go the same way and keep the same gap all along, so they never meet. Perpendicular lines meet or cross at a right angle, a square corner. Length and tilt do not matter: two slanted lines can be parallel, and two slanted lines can be perpendicular.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Parallel or perpendicular? Drag each pair of lines into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["parallel","perpendicular"],"items":[["the two rails of a train track",0],["the corner of a book",1],["the lines on lined paper",0],["the two strokes of a plus sign +",1],["the two side edges of a door",0],["the two strokes of the letter L",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagparperp","caption":"Flagship lab: turn lines to flat, upright, parallel and square-cornered, pick true parallel pairs, sort letters and draw both on a dot grid.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oDlduFnemk":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"(5, 0) lies…","choices":["on the x-axis","on the y-axis","at the origin"],"answer":0,"hint":"y = 0 means no distance up.","explain":"It is 5 across and 0 up, so it is on the x-axis."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How far is it from (2, {a}) to (2, {b})?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Same x, so subtract the y-coordinates.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} units.","params":{"a":[0,4],"b":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In (3, 7), which number tells you how far up?","choices":["7","3","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"The second number.","explain":"y comes second: 7 up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A square has corners (0, 0), ({s}, 0), ({s}, {s}) and (0, {s}). What is its perimeter?","answer":"4*s","hint":"Each side is {s} long.","explain":"4 × {s} = {=4*s} units.","params":{"s":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Are (2, 6) and (6, 2) the same point?","choices":["No: the order matters","Yes","Only on a big grid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Across first, then up.","explain":"(2, 6) is 2 across; (6, 2) is 6 across. They are different."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A rectangle has corners (1, 1), ({a}, 1), ({a}, {b}) and (1, {b}). What is its area?","answer":"(a-1)*(b-1)","hint":"Width = {a} − 1, height = {b} − 1.","explain":"{=a-1} × {=b-1} = {=(a-1)*(b-1)} square units.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"To plot (4, 1), you go…","choices":["4 across, then 1 up","4 up, then 1 across","1 across, then 4 up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Along the corridor, then up the stairs.","explain":"4 across, then 1 up."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which point is 4 across and 2 up?","choices":["(4, 2)","(2, 4)","(4, 4)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Across first.","explain":"Across first, then up: (4, 2)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How far is it from ({a}, 3) to ({b}, 3)?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Same height, so subtract the x-coordinates.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} units.","params":{"a":[0,4],"b":[5,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A point is {a} across and {b} up. What is its x-coordinate?","answer":"a","hint":"x is the across number.","explain":"The x-coordinate is {a}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Where is (0, 0)?","choices":["The origin, where the axes meet","The top right corner","Anywhere on the x-axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"Zero across, zero up.","explain":"(0, 0) is the origin."}],"explain":{"kid":["A grid has two number lines. The x-axis goes across. The y-axis goes up. They meet at the origin, which is (0, 0).","A point is written (x, y). Go across first, then up. Think: along the corridor, then up the stairs. (3, 5) means 3 across and 5 up."],"adult":["The ordered pair (x, y) gives the distance across, then the distance up, from the origin. Order matters: (2, 5) and (5, 2) are different points.","Points on the x-axis have y = 0; points on the y-axis have x = 0. Distances along a grid line are easy: from (1, 4) to (6, 4) is 6 − 1 = 5 units."]},"familyTip":"Play battleships on squared paper. Call out coordinates as (across, up).","keyIdea":"(x, y) means go x across along the x-axis, then y up, starting from the origin (0, 0).","visual":{"type":"coords","caption":"Move the point to (3, 5). Drag it, or use the arrow keys: across first, then up.","opts":{"max":8,"x":0,"y":0,"target":[3,5]}},"lab":{"type":"geolab","caption":"Drag each corner onto a grid point. Read its coordinates (across, then up), then take the area challenges.","opts":{"pts":[[1,1],[5,1],[5,4]],"grid":1,"area":6}}},"mt_Mnodea7mG_":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many vertices does a cube have?","choices":["8","4","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the corners on top and the corners at the bottom.","explain":"4 corners on top and 4 at the bottom make 8 vertices."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many faces does a square-based pyramid have?","choices":["5","4","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the triangles, then remember the square at the bottom.","explain":"4 triangle faces and 1 square face make 5 faces."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many edges does a triangular prism have?","choices":["9","6","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the edges on each triangle end, then the edges joining them.","explain":"3 edges on each triangle end makes 6, and 3 more join the ends: 9 edges."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is an edge?","choices":["A straight line where two faces meet","A corner","A flat side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of tracing in the lab.","explain":"An edge is the straight line where two flat faces meet. You traced them in red."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A cube has 12 edges. {a} of them are painted red and the rest are blue. How many edges are blue?","answer":"12-a","hint":"Start at 12 and take away the red edges.","explain":"12 − {a} = {=12-a}. {=12-a} edges are blue.","params":{"a":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which shape has 6 faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices?","choices":["A cube","A square-based pyramid","A triangular prism"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which shape is like a dice?","explain":"A cube has 6 square faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How many vertices does a ball (a sphere) have?","choices":["0","1","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a ball have any corners?","explain":"A sphere is curved all over. It has no corners, so it has 0 vertices and 0 edges."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A square-based pyramid has 5 vertices. You have put stickers on {a} of them. How many stickers do you still need?","answer":"5-a","hint":"Start at 5 and take away the stickers you have already used.","explain":"5 − {a} = {=5-a}. You need {=5-a} more stickers.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"How can you make sure you count every vertex of a box, and none twice?","choices":["Put a sticker on each one as you count it","Only count the ones at the front","Guess"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did you do in the lab?","explain":"Marking each vertex as you count it means you do not miss the ones at the back or count one twice."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is a vertex?","choices":["A corner where edges meet","A flat side","A straight line"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the stickers in the lab. Where did you put them?","explain":"A vertex is a corner. The stickers went on the corners of the cube, so a cube has 8 vertices."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A cube has 6 faces. You have painted {a} of them. How many faces are left to paint?","answer":"6-a","hint":"Start at 6 and take away the faces you have painted.","explain":"6 − {a} = {=6-a}. There are {=6-a} faces left to paint.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many edges does a cube have?","choices":["12","8","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the edges on top, the edges on the bottom and the edges going up.","explain":"4 on top, 4 on the bottom and 4 going up makes 12 edges."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A triangular prism has 9 edges. You have traced {a} of them. How many more edges do you need to trace?","answer":"9-a","hint":"Start at 9 and take away the edges you have traced.","explain":"9 − {a} = {=9-a}. You need to trace {=9-a} more edges.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Tom looks at a cube and says it has 3 faces. Why is he wrong?","choices":["Some faces are at the back, so he can not see them","A cube has no faces","He counted the edges"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see every side of a box at once?","explain":"You can only see 3 faces at once. Turn the cube and you find 3 more: 6 faces in all."}],"explain":{"kid":["A 3-D shape is a solid shape you can hold, like a box or a dice. It has parts with special names. A face is a flat side. An edge is a straight line where two faces meet. A vertex is a corner. When there is more than one, we say vertices.","A cube is like a dice. It has 6 faces, and they are all squares. It has 12 edges. It has 8 vertices: 4 on top and 4 at the bottom.","You can not see all of a shape at once. Some faces, edges and corners are at the back. Turn the shape round, or put a sticker on each part as you count it. Then you will not miss one or count one twice.","Different shapes have different numbers. A pyramid with a square bottom has 5 faces, 8 edges and 5 vertices. A triangular prism, like a tent, has 5 faces, 9 edges and 6 vertices. A ball has no edges and no vertices at all."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7 (NC Year 2: identify and describe the properties of 3-D shapes, including the number of edges, vertices and faces). Children should use the words face, edge and vertex (plural vertices) correctly, and describe a shape by its numbers: a cube has 6 faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices.","Faces here means flat faces. A cylinder has 2 flat faces and a curved surface, and a sphere has only a curved surface, so it has no edges and no vertices. In the lab all the shapes have flat faces, so every edge is a straight line where two faces meet and every vertex is where edges meet.","Common mistakes: counting only what can be seen, which gives 3 faces for a cube; counting the corners of one face, which gives 4 vertices; counting edges twice or missing the hidden ones; forgetting the base of a pyramid; and mixing up the three words. Marking each part as it is counted stops double counting.","In the lab your child puts a sticker on every vertex of a cube (one is hidden at the back), traces all 12 edges, paints the faces and turns the cube to reach the hidden ones, then counts the vertices of a square-based pyramid and the edges of a triangular prism."]},"familyTip":"Find a box, such as a cereal box or a dice. Put a small sticker or a blob of play dough on each corner and count them: these are the vertices. Run a finger along each edge, counting as you go. Then turn the box over and count the flat faces. Try the same with a Toblerone-style box (a triangular prism).","keyIdea":"We describe a 3-D shape by counting its parts. A face is a flat side. An edge is a straight line where two faces meet. A vertex is a corner where edges meet (more than one vertex are called vertices). A cube has 6 faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices. A square-based pyramid has 5 faces, 8 edges and 5 vertices. A triangular prism has 5 faces, 9 edges and 6 vertices. Some parts are round the back, so turn the shape or look for them.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a vertex, an edge or a face? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["📍 Vertex","📏 Edge","⬜ Face"],"items":[["The pointy top of a pyramid",0],["A corner of a dice",0],["The line where two sides of a box meet",1],["A straight fold along a cereal box",1],["The flat top of a dice",2],["The flat bottom of a tin can",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagevf","caption":"Flagship lab: stick a sticker on each vertex, trace every edge, paint and turn a cube, then count a pyramid and a prism.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4MFUAsbx_6":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What unit do we use to measure the size of an angle?","choices":["Degrees (°)","Centimetres","Kilograms"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the small circle after the numbers in the lab.","explain":"Angles are measured in degrees, written with the ° sign, for example 90°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"An obtuse angle's arm crosses 52 and 128. How big is it?","choices":["128°","52°","180°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Obtuse angles are between 90° and 180°.","explain":"Obtuse means more than 90°, so the angle is 128°. 52 is on the other scale."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ben reads an angle as 150°, but it looks small and pointy. What has he most likely done?","choices":["Read the wrong scale","Measured it perfectly","Used the wrong unit"],"answer":0,"hint":"A small, pointy angle is acute. What should it be less than?","explain":"An acute angle is less than 90°. 150° is on the other scale; the angle is 180 − 150 = 30°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Angle A has arms 10 cm long. Angle B has arms 3 cm long. Both measure 60°. Which is bigger?","choices":["Neither, they are the same size","A, its arms are longer","B, it looks sharper"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the length of the arms change the amount of turn between them?","explain":"Both are 60°. An angle measures the turn between the arms, not how long they are."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"An inside angle measures {a}°. How big is the reflex angle on the outside?","answer":"360-a","hint":"Inside and outside make a full turn of 360°.","explain":"360 − {a} = {=360-a}°. The inside and outside angles together make a full turn.","params":{"a":[30,170]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which reading makes sense for an obtuse angle?","choices":["125°","55°","215°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Obtuse angles are more than 90° and less than 180°.","explain":"125° is between 90° and 180°. 55° is acute and 215° is reflex."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your bottom arm points left. Which scale do you read?","choices":["The one with 0 on the left","The inner one, always","The one with the biggest numbers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which scale starts counting from your arm?","explain":"Count from the 0 that sits on your arm. No scale is always the right one."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"An arm crosses {a} on the outer scale. The angle is acute. How big is the angle?","answer":"180-a","hint":"The two numbers on the arm add to 180. An acute angle is the one less than 90°.","explain":"The two readings are {a} and {=180-a}. {a} is more than 90, so the acute angle is {=180-a}°.","params":{"a":[100,170]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"To draw an angle of 70°, what do you do after marking 70 on the right scale?","choices":["Join the mark to the vertex with a ruler","Draw a curve to the mark","Move the protractor to the mark"],"answer":0,"hint":"An angle has two straight arms that meet at the vertex.","explain":"Join the mark to the vertex with a ruler to draw the second arm. The angle between the arms is 70°."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An acute angle's arm crosses 47 and 133 on a protractor. How big is it?","choices":["47°","133°","180°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Acute angles are less than 90°. Which number fits?","explain":"Acute means less than 90°, so the angle is 47°. 133 is on the scale that starts at 180 on this arm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An arm crosses {a} on one scale of a protractor. What number does it cross on the other scale?","answer":"180-a","hint":"The two scales run opposite ways, so the two numbers always add up to 180.","explain":"180 − {a} = {=180-a}. Only one of {a} and {=180-a} is the angle: use the scale that starts at 0 on your arm.","params":{"a":[20,80]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Where does the protractor's centre mark go?","choices":["On the vertex","At the end of an arm","On the edge of the paper"],"answer":0,"hint":"The vertex is where the two arms meet.","explain":"The centre mark goes on the vertex, and the 0 line lies along one arm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The inside angle is {a}°. How big is the reflex angle that goes the long way round?","answer":"360-a","hint":"A full turn is 360°. Take away the inside angle.","explain":"360 − {a} = {=360-a}°. A reflex angle is more than 180°.","params":{"a":[40,160]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You want to draw 135° from an arm pointing right. Where do you put the mark?","choices":["At 135 on the scale that starts at 0 on the right","At 135 on the scale that starts at 0 on the left","At 45"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count up from the 0 that sits on your arm.","explain":"Use the scale whose 0 is on your arm. The other 135 mark would make a 45° angle."}],"explain":{"kid":["We measure angles in degrees. A small circle after a number means degrees, like 47°. A protractor is the tool we use.","Put the small cross in the middle of the protractor on the vertex. That is the point where the two arms meet. Then lay the 0 line along one arm.","A protractor has two rows of numbers. Use the row that starts at 0 on your arm. Count up to where the other arm crosses.","Guess first. A sharp, pointy angle is less than 90°. A wide angle is more than 90°. If your reading does not match your guess, you read the wrong row."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. This is the Year 5 geometry objective in the National Curriculum in England. Children know angles are measured in degrees. They estimate and compare acute, obtuse and reflex angles. They draw given angles, and measure them in degrees (°). Key words: angle, degree, protractor, vertex, arm, baseline, inner scale, outer scale, acute, obtuse, right angle, reflex, estimate.","The two scales run in opposite directions. So any arm crosses two numbers that add to 180. The right one is on the scale whose 0 lies on the arm you lined up. To draw an angle, mark the size on that scale. Then join the mark to the vertex with a ruler. A half-circle protractor cannot read a reflex angle directly. Measure the inside angle and take it away from 360°.","Common mistakes: reading the wrong scale (47° read as 133°); putting the centre mark at the end of an arm or on the edge, not on the vertex; starting from the protractor's edge rather than the 0 line; and thinking longer arms make a bigger angle.","In the lab your child drags a protractor onto a vertex, reads three angles where the two scales disagree, estimates three angles before checking them, draws 135° and 70°, and finds two reflex angles from their inside angles."]},"familyTip":"Draw a few angles with a ruler, swap papers and measure each other's with a protractor. Say 'acute, so less than 90' or 'obtuse, so more than 90' out loud before reading, and check that the reading agrees.","keyIdea":"Angles are measured in degrees (°) with a protractor. Put the protractor's centre mark on the vertex and line up its 0 line along one arm. A protractor has two scales, so read the one that starts at 0 on that arm, and count up to where the other arm crosses. Estimate first to check: an acute angle is less than 90°, an obtuse angle is between 90° and 180°. To draw an angle, mark the size on the same scale and join the mark to the vertex. A reflex angle is 360° minus the inside angle.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each reading sensible, or was the wrong scale used? Drag each to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Sensible reading","Wrong scale used"],"items":[["An acute angle read as 47°",0],["An acute angle read as 133°",1],["An obtuse angle read as 135°",0],["An obtuse angle read as 45°",1],["A narrow, pointy angle read as 160°",1],["A wide-open angle read as 150°",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagprot","caption":"Flagship lab: line a protractor up on the vertex, pick the right scale, estimate before measuring, draw 135° and 70°, and measure reflex angles.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_8H2kO4k2B9":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What must be true for a polygon to be regular?","choices":["All sides equal and all angles equal","All sides equal","It has a flat bottom"],"answer":0,"hint":"There are two things to check, not one.","explain":"A regular polygon has every side the same length and every angle the same size. One without the other is not enough."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A stop sign has 8 equal sides and 8 equal angles. What is its shape?","choices":["A regular octagon","An irregular octagon","A regular hexagon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the sides for the name, then check sides and angles.","explain":"8 sides makes it an octagon. All sides and angles are equal, so it is a regular octagon."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A triangle has sides of 5 cm, 5 cm and 3 cm. Is it regular?","choices":["No, one side is different","Yes, two sides match","Yes, all triangles are regular"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every side must match, not just two of them.","explain":"One side is 3 cm, so the sides are not all equal. Only an equilateral triangle is a regular triangle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which quadrilateral is regular?","choices":["A square","A rectangle","A rhombus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has equal sides and equal angles?","explain":"A square has 4 equal sides and 4 right angles. A rectangle has unequal sides; a rhombus has unequal angles."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia says a big regular pentagon is more regular than a small one. Is she right?","choices":["No, size does not matter","Yes, bigger is more regular","Yes, small shapes cannot be regular"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does making a shape bigger change whether its sides and angles match?","explain":"Both pentagons have 5 equal sides and 5 equal angles. Size does not change regularity."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A regular octagon has sides of {a} cm. What is the total length of its sides?","answer":"8*a","hint":"An octagon has 8 sides, and in a regular one they are all equal.","explain":"8 sides × {a} cm = {=8*a} cm, because every side of a regular octagon is equal.","params":{"a":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Is a circle a regular polygon?","choices":["No, it has no straight sides","Yes, it is perfectly even","Yes, it has one equal side"],"answer":0,"hint":"What are polygons made of?","explain":"A polygon is made of straight sides. A circle has none, so it is not a polygon."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"An eight-sided shape has sides of 3 cm and 2 cm. What is it?","choices":["An irregular octagon","A regular octagon","Not an octagon"],"answer":0,"hint":"The number of sides gives the name. The side lengths tell you regular or not.","explain":"It has 8 sides, so it is an octagon. Its sides are not all equal, so it is irregular."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"The sides of a regular pentagon add up to {=5*a} cm. How long is one side?","answer":"a","hint":"A regular pentagon has 5 sides that are all the same length.","explain":"{=5*a} cm ÷ 5 = {a} cm. Every side is the same because the pentagon is regular.","params":{"a":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A rectangle is 6 cm long and 3 cm wide. Is it regular?","choices":["No, its sides are not all equal","Yes, all its angles are 90°","Yes, it has 4 sides"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the sides as well as the angles.","explain":"All 4 angles are 90°, but the sides are 6 cm and 3 cm. Both sides and angles must be equal, so it is irregular."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A regular hexagon has sides of {a} cm. What is the total length of all its sides?","answer":"6*a","hint":"A regular hexagon has 6 sides, and they are all the same length.","explain":"6 sides × {a} cm = {=6*a} cm, because every side of a regular hexagon is equal.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A rhombus has 4 sides of 5 cm. Two angles are 60° and two are 120°. Is it regular?","choices":["No, its angles are not all equal","Yes, its sides are all equal","Yes, it has 4 corners"],"answer":0,"hint":"Equal sides are only half of the test.","explain":"The sides match, but the angles are 60° and 120°. A regular polygon needs equal angles too, so this rhombus is irregular."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A regular polygon has {n} sides. They add up to {=n*a} cm. How long is each side?","answer":"a","hint":"In a regular polygon every side is the same. Share the total equally between the sides.","explain":"{=n*a} cm ÷ {n} sides = {a} cm. Every side of a regular polygon is the same length.","params":{"n":[3,8],"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A square is turned so that it stands on one corner. Is it still regular?","choices":["Yes, its sides and angles have not changed","No, it is tilted now","Only if you turn it back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does turning a shape change any side length or angle?","explain":"Turning moves the shape but does not change its sides or angles. It still has 4 equal sides and 4 right angles, so it is regular."}],"explain":{"kid":["A polygon is a flat shape with straight sides, like a triangle or a hexagon.","A polygon is regular when every side is the same length and every angle is the same size. You need both.","A rhombus has equal sides, but its corners are not all the same. So it is irregular. A rectangle has equal corners, but its sides are not all the same. So it is irregular too.","A square is regular, even if you tip it onto a corner. Turning a shape or making it bigger does not change its sides or angles."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. This is a Year 5 geometry aim in the National Curriculum in England. Children tell regular and irregular polygons apart. They reason about equal sides and equal angles. Key words: polygon, regular, irregular, side, angle, vertex, rhombus, rectangle, square, pentagon, hexagon, octagon.","A regular polygon has all sides equal and all angles equal. One rule alone is not enough. A rhombus has equal sides but not equal angles. A rectangle has equal angles but not equal sides. So the square is the only regular four-sided shape. The equilateral triangle is the only regular triangle. Turning or resizing a shape never changes this.","Common mistakes: calling a rectangle regular because its angles match; calling a rhombus regular because its sides match; thinking a tilted square is no longer regular; and using the number of sides as the test, when it only gives the name. A circle is not a polygon at all. It has no straight sides.","In the lab your child drags one corner of a pentagon until its numbers all match. Then they squash a rhombus into a square and stretch a rectangle into a square. They sort eight shapes using the numbers. Last, they turn and resize a regular hexagon and see that it stays regular."]},"familyTip":"Go on a shape hunt: a stop sign, a floor tile, a honeycomb picture, a door, a slice of pizza. For each one ask two questions out loud: are all the sides the same length? Are all the corners the same? Only two yeses make it regular.","keyIdea":"A polygon is a flat shape with straight sides. It is regular when all its sides are the same length and all its angles are the same size. If even one side or one angle is different, it is irregular. A rhombus has equal sides but unequal angles, and a rectangle has equal angles but unequal sides, so both are irregular unless they are squares. Turning a shape or changing its size does not change whether it is regular.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Regular or irregular? Drag each shape to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Regular","Irregular"],"items":[["A stop sign: 8 equal sides, 8 equal angles",0],["A square standing on one corner",0],["A rhombus with angles of 60° and 120°",1],["A rectangle 6 cm by 3 cm",1],["A triangle with sides 5, 5 and 3 cm",1],["A honeycomb cell: 6 equal sides, 6 angles of 120°",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagregpol","caption":"Flagship lab: fix a pentagon, turn a rhombus and a rectangle into squares, sort shapes, then turn and grow a hexagon.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XjwUlmxdCT":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What can two same triangles make, with their long sides touching?","choices":["A rectangle","A circle","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a sandwich cut corner to corner. Put it back together.","explain":"Two triangles with their long sides touching make a rectangle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Four squares in a 2 by 2 block make…","choices":["A bigger square","A long rectangle","A triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it as tall as it is wide?","explain":"It is 2 squares wide and 2 squares tall, so all 4 sides match: a bigger square."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which block will not fit in a hexagon with no gaps?","choices":["A square","A triangle","A trapezoid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the corners of each block.","explain":"A square has square corners that do not match the hexagon's corners, so it leaves gaps."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Each house picture uses 1 square and 1 triangle. How many shapes do you need for {n} houses?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each house needs 2 shapes: a square and a triangle.","explain":"{n} houses with 2 shapes each need {=2*n} shapes.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Can a hexagon be filled in more than one way?","choices":["Yes, with different blocks","No, only one way","Only with squares"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try 6 triangles, then 2 trapezoids.","explain":"6 triangles, 3 rhombuses or 2 trapezoids all fill the same hexagon."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Two triangles joined along a short side can make…","choices":["A bigger triangle","A circle","A hexagon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put two corner pieces back to back. Count the sides.","explain":"Joined along a short side, two right-angled triangles can make a bigger triangle."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A big shape is made of {n} squares and {k} triangles. How many small shapes is that?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Add the squares and the triangles.","explain":"{n} squares and {k} triangles make {=n+k} small shapes in one big shape.","params":{"n":[1,4],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A triangle will not fit. What should you try first?","choices":["Turn it round","Get a circle","Push it in hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, what made the triangles fit the rectangle?","explain":"Turning the triangle can make its sides line up with the space, and it is still a triangle."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Two squares are put side by side. What shape do they make?","choices":["A rectangle","A bigger square","A triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the new shape. Are all its sides the same length?","explain":"Two sides are long and two are short, so it is a rectangle, not a bigger square."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"It takes 2 triangles to make one rectangle. How many triangles do you need to make {n} rectangles?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count 2 triangles for each rectangle.","explain":"{n} rectangles with 2 triangles in each need {=2*n} triangles.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Two triangles touch at just one corner. Have they made one new shape?","choices":["No, a full side must touch","Yes","Yes, if they are close"],"answer":0,"hint":"Shapes join when a whole side touches a whole side.","explain":"Touching at one corner is not joined. Slide them so a full side touches."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A hexagon is filled by 6 small triangles. You have put in {k}. How many more triangles do you need?","answer":"6-k","hint":"6 triangles fill the hexagon. Take away the ones you have put in.","explain":"6 take away {k} is {=6-k} more triangles to fill the hexagon with no gaps.","params":{"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You turn a triangle so it points down. What shape is it now?","choices":["Still a triangle","A new shape","A square"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count its sides and corners after you turn it.","explain":"Turning does not change a shape. It still has 3 sides and 3 corners, so it is a triangle."}],"explain":{"kid":["Small shapes can join to make a bigger shape. Two triangles can make a rectangle. Put their long sides together, and the rectangle is full.","Two squares side by side make a rectangle. It is not a bigger square, because two sides are long and two are short. Four squares in a block make a bigger square.","To join, a whole side must touch a whole side. If two shapes touch at just one corner, or have a gap, they are still two shapes.","Sometimes a shape will only fit if you turn it. A turned triangle is still a triangle. And you can fill the same big shape in lots of ways, like a hexagon made of 6 triangles or 2 trapezoids."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 6. This is CCSS K.G.6: compose simple shapes to form larger shapes, for example joining two triangles with full sides touching to make a rectangle. It builds on naming 2-D shapes and leads to Grade 1 composing shapes (1.G.2) and later to area and fractions of shapes. Key words: compose, combine, join, side, corner (vertex), turn (rotate), triangle, square, rectangle, rhombus, trapezoid (trapezium in the UK), hexagon.","Pattern blocks make the relations exact: a hexagon equals 6 green triangles, 3 blue rhombuses or 2 red trapezoids, so it can be filled in several ways. A square never fits, as its right angles cannot match the hexagon's 120 degree corners. Children move from placing shapes one by one by trial and error to planning which pieces fit, and that shift is what the turning and filling tasks practise.","Common mistakes: thinking two squares make a bigger square; thinking a shape turned on its side has become a different shape; joining shapes at a corner or with a gap and calling it one shape; believing there is only one right way to fill a shape; and trying to force a piece in without turning it. The lab, the practice and the challenge target each of these.","In the lab your child turns two triangles and drags them into a rectangle; pushes together two squares, two triangles and then four squares and names each new shape; fills a hexagon with pattern blocks in two different ways; drags a square, a triangle, a rectangle and a circle into a house picture; and slides a blue piece until a full side touches, so the two pieces make a rectangle and then a square."]},"familyTip":"Cut a square of paper corner to corner to make two triangles. Ask your child to put them back into a square, then make a rectangle, then a big triangle. Talk about which sides touch. Toast, sandwiches and building blocks work too: how many ways can you make a bigger shape from two pieces?","keyIdea":"Small shapes can join to make bigger shapes. Two triangles, with their long sides touching, make a rectangle. Two squares side by side make a rectangle, and four squares make a bigger square. Shapes only join into one new shape when a full side touches a full side: touching at a corner, or leaving a gap, does not count. You may need to turn a shape to make it fit, and turning it does not change what shape it is. The same big shape can often be made in more than one way.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Do the pieces make one new shape? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["One new shape","Still two shapes"],"items":[["Two squares, full sides touching",0],["Two squares touching at a corner",1],["Two triangles, long sides touching",0],["Two triangles with a gap",1],["Four squares in a 2 by 2 block",0],["A circle next to a square",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcompose","caption":"Flagship lab: turn two triangles to fill a rectangle, join and name new shapes, fill a hexagon two ways, build a picture, join full sides.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_RrmTYzKeFl":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A triangle has sides 7, 24 and 25. Is it right-angled?","choices":["Yes, because 49 + 576 = 625 = 25²","No, because 7 + 24 is not 25","You cannot tell without measuring an angle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Square the two shorter sides and add, then compare with 25².","explain":"7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625 and 25² = 625. They are equal, so by the converse the angle opposite 25 is a right angle. Adding the sides themselves proves nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sides 10, 26 and 24. Which check uses the converse correctly?","choices":["10² + 24² = 26²","10² + 26² = 24²","10² + 24² + 26² = 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"c must be the longest side, wherever it appears in the list.","explain":"26 is the longest side, so check 10² + 24² = 100 + 576 = 676 = 26². It is right-angled, with the right angle opposite the 26 side."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A builder marks 3 m and 4 m along two walls and finds the gap is 5 m. What has she used?","choices":["The converse: the sides fit, so the corner is 90°","The theorem: the corner is 90°, so the sides fit","Neither, it is just a guess"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did she start from a known right angle, or from three lengths?","explain":"She started from three measured lengths and concluded the corner is square. Going from a² + b² = c² to a right angle is the converse."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A right triangle has legs a = {a} and b = {b}. In the rearrangement proof, what is the side of the big square?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Each side of the big square is made of one short side a and one short side b.","explain":"Each edge of the big square runs along a leg a and a leg b, so its side is {a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"In the rearrangement proof with legs a = {a} and b = {b}, what is the total area of the four triangles?","answer":"2*a*b","hint":"Each triangle has area ½ × {a} × {b}. There are four.","explain":"4 × ½ × {a} × {b} = 2 × {a} × {b} = {=2*a*b}. This is the same in both pictures, which is why the gaps match.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sides 5, 6 and 8. What kind of triangle is it?","choices":["Obtuse","Right-angled","Acute"],"answer":0,"hint":"Square the longest side, 8, and compare it with 5² + 6².","explain":"5² + 6² = 61 and 8² = 64. c² is bigger than a² + b², so the angle opposite 8 is more than 90°: obtuse."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"'If a shape is a square, it has four right angles' is true. What about its converse?","choices":["False: a rectangle has four right angles but may not be a square","True, because the converse of a true statement is always true","It is the same statement"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find a shape with four right angles that is not a square.","explain":"A 2 by 5 rectangle has four right angles but is not a square, so the converse is false. That is why Pythagoras' converse needs its own proof."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which step would make a proof of Pythagoras' theorem circular?","choices":["Saying the gaps match because a² + b² = c²","Saying the big square has side a + b","Saying each triangle has area ½ab"],"answer":0,"hint":"Circular means using the result you are trying to prove.","explain":"a² + b² = c² is what the proof sets out to show, so it cannot be a reason inside the proof. The gaps match because both are the big square minus the same four triangles."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In the rearrangement proof, why is the uncovered area the same in both pictures?","choices":["Both are the same big square minus the same four triangles","Because a² + b² = c²","The two pictures look about the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what stays the same when the triangles slide: the outer square and the pieces.","explain":"The big square has side a + b both times, and the four triangles are the same. Equal total minus equal pieces leaves equal gaps, so c² = a² + b². Using a² + b² = c² as the reason would be circular."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A triangle has sides {=3*k}, {=4*k} and {=5*k}. Work out a² + b² using the two shorter sides.","answer":"25*k*k","hint":"Square {=3*k} and {=4*k}, then add. Compare with {=5*k}².","explain":"{=3*k}² + {=4*k}² = {=9*k*k} + {=16*k*k} = {=25*k*k}, which equals {=5*k}², so by the converse the triangle is right-angled.","params":{"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which statement is the converse of Pythagoras' theorem?","choices":["If a² + b² = c² for the longest side c, then the angle opposite c is 90°","If a triangle has a right angle, then a² + b² = c²","If a triangle has no right angle, then a + b = c"],"answer":0,"hint":"A converse swaps the 'if' part and the 'then' part.","explain":"The theorem says right angle → a² + b² = c². The converse swaps them: a² + b² = c² → right angle. The second choice is the theorem itself."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Sides 6, 8 and {c}. Work out c² − (a² + b²), using {c} as the longest side.","answer":"c*c-100","hint":"6² + 8² = 100. Square {c} and subtract 100.","explain":"{c}² − 100 = {=c*c-100}. Only 0 would mean a right angle; a positive answer means the angle opposite {c} is obtuse.","params":{"c":[11,16]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Take one right triangle with short sides a and b and long side c. Make four copies. Put them in the corners of a big square with side a + b. The gap in the middle is a tilted square. Each of its sides is c, so its area is c².","Now slide the same four triangles into new places in the same big square. Pair them up into two rectangles. This time the gaps are two squares: one is a by a and the other is b by b. Their total area is a² + b².","Why must the gaps match? Both times the gap is the big square minus the same four triangles. Nothing was added or lost. So c² = a² + b². This works for any right triangle, not just one you drew.","The converse flips the order. You start with three side lengths and ask if there is a right angle. Square the two shorter sides and add. If that equals the square of the longest side, the angle opposite the longest side is 90°. Sides 9, 12 and 15 pass: 81 + 144 = 225 = 15²."],"adult":["This lesson covers the GCSE Mathematics content in England on Pythagoras' theorem and the US Common Core standard 8.G.B.6: explain a proof of the Pythagorean Theorem and its converse. Learners explain an area (rearrangement) proof, state the theorem and its converse as two separate conditional statements, and use the converse to decide whether a triangle with given sides is right-angled.","Key terms: hypotenuse, right angle, conditional statement, converse, proof, rearrangement, area, and Pythagorean triple. The theorem has the form 'if the angle is 90°, then a² + b² = c²'. The converse has the form 'if a² + b² = c², then the angle opposite c is 90°'. Both are true, but each needs its own proof; the converse can be proved by building a right triangle with legs a and b and showing it is congruent (SSS) to the given one.","Common mistakes: squaring the first two numbers listed instead of the two shorter sides, so 13, 5 and 12 is wrongly rejected. Adding the sides (7 + 24 = 31) instead of their squares. Treating a picture as a proof without saying why the areas are equal. Using a² + b² = c² inside the proof itself, which is circular. Thinking a true statement makes its converse true; 'a square has four right angles' is true, but its converse is false.","In the lab the learner slides three triangles so the uncovered c² becomes a² and b², orders the four lines of the area argument, sorts real jobs into theorem or converse, tests four side lists with the longest side as c, and then uses c² compared with a² + b² to name triangles as acute, right or obtuse."]},"familyTip":"Use a tape measure to check a corner at home. Mark 30 cm along one edge and 40 cm along the other. If the gap between the marks is exactly 50 cm, the corner is square. Builders use this 3-4-5 check, and it is the converse of Pythagoras at work.","keyIdea":"Pythagoras' theorem says: if a triangle has a right angle, then a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse. One proof puts four copies of the triangle inside a square of side a + b in two ways. The gap left over is c² one way and a² + b² the other, and both gaps are the same big square minus the same four triangles. The converse runs the other way: if a² + b² = c² for the longest side c, then the angle opposite c is a right angle.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each triangle right-angled? Square the two shorter sides, add, and compare with the square of the longest side. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Right-angled","Not right-angled"],"items":[["Sides 9, 12, 15",0],["Sides 7, 24, 25",0],["Sides 13, 5, 12",0],["Sides 6, 8, 11",1],["Sides 8, 15, 16",1],["Sides 5, 6, 8",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpproof","caption":"Flagship lab: slide four triangles to turn c² into a² + b², order the proof, sort theorem from converse, test side lists, and name acute, right or obtuse.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
